{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01FdVRtECVUBu8UYVNhiUSuM","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01L2FpcWUgMTemnquSFy5nXB","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/Users/philip/projects/hw-budget-tests/plan-gen-execution/agentRoot/source/hankweave/standard-operating-procedures.md"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3799,"cache_creation_input_tokens":21614,"cache_read_input_tokens":47742,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":21614},"output_tokens":323,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"6f6abf3a-0b5f-4251-a578-7f2d66ac9b9a","uuid":"dab4d360-d818-4b8d-95b7-40434069972a","timestamp":"2026-03-05T12:12:34.885Z"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01L2FpcWUgMTemnquSFy5nXB","type":"tool_result","content":"     1→# Standard Operating Procedures\n     2→\n     3→This document captures the development conventions, release workflow, and operational norms for the Hankweave codebase. Written for humans and AI agents working in the repo.\n     4→\n     5→Last updated: 2026-02-14 (v0.4.1).\n     6→\n     7→---\n     8→\n     9→## 1. Repository Structure\n    10→\n    11→```\n    12→hankweave-dev/              ← private repo (SouthBridgeAI/hankweave)\n    13→├── server/                 ← runtime source code (TypeScript, Bun)\n    14→├── scripts/                ← build, release, sync, and dev tooling\n    15→├── tests/                  ← unit, integration, e2e, cross-runtime\n    16→├── schemas/                ← generated JSON schemas (from Zod)\n    17→├── shims/                  ← agent harness adapters\n    18→├── learning/               ← example hanks and guides (ships to public)\n    19→├── intermediates/          ← planning docs, numbered by feature (internal only)\n    20→├── external-docs/          ← SDK reference docs for agent context (internal only)\n    21→├── public-release-files/   ← files overlaid onto the public repo during sync\n    22→├── assets/                 ← images, demo GIFs\n    23→├── .github/                ← CI workflows and composite actions\n    24→├── CHANGELOG.md            ← Keep a Changelog format, [Unreleased] workflow\n    25→├── CONTRIBUTING.md         ← branch model and PR guidelines (internal only)\n    26→└── package.json            ← @southbridgeai/hankweave (private npm)\n    27→```\n    28→\n    29→There is a separate public repo at `SouthBridgeAI/hankweave-runtime` which receives sanitized snapshots via the sync workflow. It publishes the public `hankweave` npm package.\n    30→\n    31→### What gets stripped before public release\n    32→\n    33→The sync process (`scripts/sync-public.ts`) removes internal-only content:\n    34→\n    35→- **Directories**: `intermediates/`, `external-docs/`, `public-release-files/`, `.github/actions/`\n    36→- **Files**: `CLAUDE.md`, `CONTRIBUTING.md`, `PUBLISHING-PLAN.md`, `telemetry.md` (root), `reference/telemetry.md` (internal version), `.github/workflows/ci.yml`, `.github/workflows/sync-public.yml`, `scripts/sync-public.ts`, `scripts/test-sync-local.ts`, `scripts/release.ts`, `.DS_Store`\n    37→\n    38→The `public-release-files/` directory contains replacements that get overlaid: a public-facing `README.md`, `LICENSE.md`, `NOTICE.md`, a sanitized `reference/telemetry.md`, and a simplified `.github/workflows/release.yml`.\n    39→\n    40→The `package.json` is transformed: name becomes `hankweave`, `publishConfig.access` becomes `public`, `homepage` points to the public repo, and `release:*` scripts are removed.\n    41→\n    42→---\n    43→\n    44→## 2. Branch Model\n    45→\n    46→```\n    47→feature branches → develop → release/alpha\n    48→                      ↑\n    49→              all PRs target here\n    50→```\n    51→\n    52→- **`develop`** — active development. All PRs target this branch. All work starts here.\n    53→- **`release/alpha`** — the default branch on GitHub. Customer-facing. Tagged releases are cut from here. Never commit directly to release/alpha; it receives merges from develop only during the release process.\n    54→- **No `main` or `master` branch.** This is intentional.\n    55→\n    56→### Feature branch naming\n    57→\n    58→- Author-prefixed: `hrishi/sentinel-qol-fixes`, `hrishi/init-wizard`\n    59→- Descriptive: `fix/loop-iteration-telemetry`, `shim-timeout`\n    60→- Ticket-based: `ENG-90`\n    61→\n    62→The `hrishi/*` prefix is Hrishi's convention for easier identification. Philip uses bare names. Either is fine.\n    63→\n    64→---\n    65→\n    66→## 3. Development Workflow\n    67→\n    68→### The four-eyes principle\n    69→\n    70→We operate on a **four-eyes principle** (ideally six, counting agents). The workflow for non-trivial changes:\n    71→\n    72→1. **Plan** — discuss the approach, often with an AI agent. Write a plan in `intermediates/<number>-<name>/plan.md` for larger features.\n    73→2. **Review the plan** — read it critically. Replan if needed.\n    74→3. **Implement** — have an agent write the code, or write it yourself.\n    75→4. **Lint and test locally** — `bun run lint`, `bun run tc`, then tests (see testing order below).\n    76→5. **Review the code line by line** — the author reads every line before pushing.\n    77→6. **Push as a PR** — target `develop`. Include ticket numbers in the title.\n    78→7. **Peer review** — another team member reviews before merging (~90% of the time, depending on size and risk).\n    79→8. **Squash merge** — standard merge method. Keeps develop history clean.\n    80→\n    81→**Less code is more.** Hankweave has concurrent components and is deployed across multiple environments. Every line should be considered for its implications. Get as much reviewed locally before opening the PR.\n    82→\n    83→ALL changes have at least two pairs of eyes reviewing them, and have enough deliberation. Often a review is requested from whoever is the implicit owner or originator of a section.\n    84→\n    85→### Intermediates\n    86→\n    87→The `intermediates/` directory holds planning documents for features and investigations. Numbered sequentially (not tied to Linear tickets). Structure varies:\n    88→\n    89→- Single file: `intermediates/7-cleanup-integration-improvements.md`\n    90→- Multi-phase: `intermediates/12-execution-isolation/1-plan.md` → `2-test-fixes.md` → `3-start-new.md` → `4-completed.md`\n    91→- Bug reports: `intermediates/39-QoL-3/BUG_REPORT_HEADLESS_HTTP_CRASH.md`\n    92→\n    93→These are stripped from release/alpha and the public repo.\n    94→\n    95→### External docs\n    96→\n    97→`external-docs/` holds SDK documentation and reference material (Claude Code SDK, AI SDK, Gemini CLI, etc.). These exist primarily so AI agents have context when working on integrations. Stripped before release.\n    98→\n    99→---\n   100→\n   101→## 4. PR Conventions\n   102→\n   103→### Title format\n   104→\n   105→Include Linear ticket numbers when applicable:\n   106→\n   107→```\n   108→Fixes: Sentinel drains, telemetry fixes, rollback QoL (ENG-194, ENG-193)\n   109→Quality-of-Life Improvements Batch 3 (ENG-158, ENG-180, ENG-179)\n   110→CC not exiting Bug (ENG-186)\n   111→```\n   112→\n   113→Linear is connected to GitHub — including the ticket number in the PR title updates the issue status when the PR merges.\n   114→\n   115→### Body format\n   116→\n   117→PR bodies are detailed. For each change, include:\n   118→\n   119→- **Problem** — what was wrong or what was needed\n   120→- **Fix/Approach** — what was done and why\n   121→- **Files** — which files were touched\n   122→\n   123→Batched PRs (multiple related changes) number each change. See PR #110 for the canonical example (22 numbered sections covering sentinel fixes, telemetry, TUI improvements, docs, and tests).\n   124→\n   125→### Changelog entries\n   126→\n   127→Changelog entries are typically **assembled in the PR body first**, then cleaned up or redacted at release time. The format is:\n   128→\n   129→```markdown\n   130→- **Bold feature/fix name** — Description covering the what and why in one sentence.\n   131→```\n   132→\n   133→Examples from the codebase:\n   134→\n   135→```markdown\n   136→- **Sentinels watching `codon.completed` now reliably fire** — Added a second sentinel queue drain after the `codon.completed` event emission.\n   137→- **Remote hank cache: hash-based freshness** — Branch caches now validate via `git ls-remote` instead of a 1-hour TTL.\n   138→```\n   139→\n   140→### Labels and milestones\n   141→\n   142→Not actively used. Tracking lives in Linear.\n   143→\n   144→### Merge method\n   145→\n   146→**Squash merge** is the standard. This collapses the branch into a single commit on develop with the PR title as the message.\n   147→\n   148→Rebasing is used when working on sequential changes on the same branch. Force-push to a feature branch is okay if you're sure nobody else is on it. Otherwise, cross-merge.\n   149→\n   150→### Attribution and accountability\n   151→\n   152→- **Humans are responsible for all activity.** Nothing is committed or written that hasn't been read end-to-end by a human. Agents operate under their human's supervision and responsibility.\n   153→- **Always quote AI agents.** If a PR body, plan, or document was written by an agent, say so (e.g., \"generated by Opus 4.6, reviewed by @hrishioa\"). If an agent produced a code change, the PR should note which parts were agent-generated. See PR #113 for an example.\n   154→- **Do not add AI tools as committers** in git commits. The human who reviewed and approved the work is the author.\n   155→\n   156→---\n   157→\n   158→## 5. Code Style\n   159→\n   160→- **Language**: TypeScript, ES2022 target, strict mode\n   161→- **Runtime**: Bun (for execution, package management, and testing)\n   162→- **Formatter/Linter**: Biome 2.x — 2-space indent, 100-char line width, LF endings\n   163→  - Covers: `server/`, `tests/e2e/`, `tests/utils/`, `tests/unit/`\n   164→  - Does not cover: `scripts/`, `tests/integration/`, `learning/`\n   165→- **Line endings**: LF everywhere (enforced by `.gitattributes`)\n   166→- **Type checking**: `bun run tc` (runs `tsc --noEmit`)\n   167→\n   168→Run before pushing:\n   169→\n   170→```bash\n   171→bun run lint      # biome check\n   172→bun run tc        # typecheck\n   173→```\n   174→\n   175→---\n   176→\n   177→## 6. Testing\n   178→\n   179→### Test hierarchy (run in this order)\n   180→\n   181→1. **Unit tests** — `bun run test:unit` — fast, no external deps, mock-based\n   182→2. **Integration tests** — `bun run test:integration` — component interactions, mock LLM providers, no API keys needed\n   183→3. **E2E init tests** — `bun run test:e2e:init` — tests the `--init` scaffolding flow (needs real harness binaries)\n   184→4. **Full E2E** — `bun run test` — includes happy-path and rollback tests (needs API keys: Claude, Gemini, Codex)\n   185→\n   186→Tests can be expensive (real LLM calls in E2E). Start with unit, widen as confidence is needed.\n   187→\n   188→### CI matrix\n   189→\n   190→CI (`.github/workflows/ci.yml`) runs on every push to `develop` and PRs targeting it:\n   191→\n   192→- **Lint + typecheck** on ubuntu\n   193→- **Unit + integration** on ubuntu, windows, macos\n   194→- **Init E2E** (normal, npx, binary modes) on ubuntu, windows, macos\n   195→- Uses composite actions in `.github/actions/` for environment setup, test execution, and log dumping on failure\n   196→- E2E tests have retry logic (3 attempts, 10s between retries) because harness processes can be flaky\n   197→\n   198→### Test area\n   199→\n   200→Tests use `tests/test-area/` for temporary files and `tests/test-results/` for output. Both are gitignored.\n   201→\n   202→---\n   203→\n   204→## 7. Release Process\n   205→\n   206→### Scripts\n   207→\n   208→| Script                        | Purpose                                                          |\n   209→| ----------------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------- |\n   210→| `scripts/release.ts`          | Version bump, changelog stamp, merge to release/alpha, tag, push |\n   211→| `scripts/sync-public.ts`      | Snapshot private repo → transform → push to public repo          |\n   212→| `scripts/test-sync-local.ts`  | Local dry-run of the sync process                                |\n   213→| `scripts/build.ts`            | Build the npm package (dist/)                                    |\n   214→| `scripts/build-executable.ts` | Build standalone Bun binaries for 5 platforms                    |\n   215→| `scripts/generate-schemas.ts` | Generate JSON schemas from Zod types                             |\n   216→\n   217→### Release flow (private → npm + binaries)\n   218→\n   219→```bash\n   220→# 1. Dry-run validation (recommended before every release)\n   221→bun scripts/release.ts patch --dry-run\n   222→\n   223→# 2. Interactive release\n   224→bun scripts/release.ts minor\n   225→\n   226→# 3. Headless release (for agents)\n   227→bun scripts/release.ts patch --yes\n   228→```\n   229→\n   230→The release script has two phases:\n   231→\n   232→**Phase 1 — Validation** (no changes made):\n   233→\n   234→- Checks branch (must be develop), clean tree, remote sync\n   235→- Computes new version, checks tag availability\n   236→- Validates CHANGELOG has entries in [Unreleased]\n   237→- Tests that develop → release/alpha merge would succeed (no conflicts)\n   238→- Tests schema generation works\n   239→- Runs typecheck and lint (skip with `--skip-checks`)\n   240→- Prints a plan summary\n   241→\n   242→**Phase 2 — Execute** (with automatic rollback on failure):\n   243→\n   244→1. Bump version in package.json\n   245→2. Stamp [Unreleased] → [version] - date in CHANGELOG\n   246→3. Regenerate JSON schemas\n   247→4. Commit \"Release vX.Y.Z\" on develop\n   248→5. Add empty [Unreleased] template, commit\n   249→6. Push develop\n   250→7. Checkout release/alpha, merge develop, strip internal files (intermediates/, external-docs/, CLAUDE.md, CONTRIBUTING.md)\n   251→8. Create tag on release/alpha\n   252→9. Push release/alpha + tag (triggers CI)\n   253→10. Return to develop\n   254→\n   255→If any step fails after changes begin, the script attempts automatic rollback (reset branches to saved checkpoints, delete tags). If changes already reached remote, it prints manual recovery commands.\n   256→\n   257→### What CI does on tag push (private repo)\n   258→\n   259→`.github/workflows/release.yml` triggers on `v*` tags:\n   260→\n   261→1. **publish-npm** — typecheck, lint, tests, then `npm publish` as `@southbridgeai/hankweave` (restricted)\n   262→2. **build-executables** — builds Bun binaries for linux-x64, linux-arm64, darwin-x64, darwin-arm64, windows-x64, with smoke tests on testable platforms\n   263→3. **create-release** — downloads artifacts, creates GitHub release with binaries and changelog-derived release notes\n   264→\n   265→### Sync to public\n   266→\n   267→After the private release CI passes, manually trigger the \"Sync to Public\" workflow on GitHub:\n   268→\n   269→```\n   270→Actions → Sync to Public → Run workflow → version: 0.4.1\n   271→```\n   272→\n   273→Or test locally first:\n   274→\n   275→```bash\n   276→# Dry run (no push)\n   277→bun scripts/test-sync-local.ts --version 0.4.1\n   278→\n   279→# Actually push\n   280→bun scripts/test-sync-local.ts --version 0.4.1 --push\n   281→```\n   282→\n   283→The sync:\n   284→\n   285→1. Checks out the private repo at the version tag\n   286→2. Clones the public repo's release/alpha branch\n   287→3. Builds a snapshot: rsync (excluding internal files) → overlay public-release-files → remove ci.yml → transform package.json\n   288→4. Extracts changelog (aggregates all versions since last public release)\n   289→5. Replaces the public repo's tree with the snapshot\n   290→6. Commits, tags, pushes to public repo\n   291→\n   292→The public repo's tag push triggers its own `release.yml`: typecheck, lint, build, `npm publish --access public` as `hankweave`, build binaries, create GitHub release.\n   293→\n   294→### Version guidance\n   295→\n   296→- **patch** — bug fixes, docs, minor improvements\n   297→- **minor** — new features, behavioral changes, UX overhauls\n   298→- **major** — breaking changes (hasn't happened yet, pre-1.0)\n   299→\n   300→---\n   301→\n   302→## 8. CHANGELOG Conventions\n   303→\n   304→Format follows [Keep a Changelog](https://keepachangelog.com/). Structure:\n   305→\n   306→```markdown\n   307→## [Unreleased]\n   308→\n   309→### Added\n   310→\n   311→- **Feature name** — Description.\n   312→\n   313→### Changed\n   314→\n   315→- **Change name** — Description.\n   316→\n   317→### Fixed\n   318→\n   319→- **Fix name** — Description.\n   320→\n   321→## [0.4.1] - 2026-02-13\n   322→\n   323→...\n   324→```\n   325→\n   326→Rules:\n   327→\n   328→- Always have an `[Unreleased]` section at the top\n   329→- Entries use **bold name** — description format\n   330→- Group entries under Added/Changed/Fixed subsections\n   331→- The release script stamps [Unreleased] → [version] - date automatically\n   332→- Entries are assembled during PR work, cleaned up at release time\n   333→- If a version wasn't synced to public, it can be consolidated (e.g., v0.4.0 content was rolled into v0.4.1 because v0.4.0 was internal-only)\n   334→\n   335→---\n   336→\n   337→## 9. Key Files Reference\n   338→\n   339→| File                                   | Purpose                                                                       |\n   340→| -------------------------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n   341→| `server/index.ts`                      | CLI entry point — arg parsing, routing to commands                            |\n   342→| `server/hankweave-runtime.ts`          | Core runtime — codon execution, state management, event emission              |\n   343→| `server/config.ts`                     | 5-layer config resolution (defaults → hankweave.json → hank.json → env → CLI) |\n   344→| `server/basic-tui.ts`                  | Built-in terminal UI (WebSocket client)                                       |\n   345→| `server/codon-runner.ts`               | Runs individual codons via harness managers                                   |\n   346→| `server/claude-agent-sdk-manager.ts`   | Claude Code SDK integration                                                   |\n   347→| `server/shim-process-manager.ts`       | Process manager for shim-based harnesses (Codex, Gemini)                      |\n   348→| `server/remote-hank.ts`                | Git-based remote hank fetching and caching                                    |\n   349→| `server/sentinels/`                    | Sentinel system (event-triggered observers)                                   |\n   350→| `server/telemetry/`                    | Anonymous usage telemetry (PostHog)                                           |\n   351→| `server/schemas/event-schemas.ts`      | WebSocket event type definitions (Zod)                                        |\n   352→| `server/config-validation/`            | Validation schemas for hank files and configs                                 |\n   353→| `tests/utils/sentinel-test-harness.ts` | Shared test utilities for sentinel tests                                      |\n   354→| `tests/utils/mock-llm.ts`              | Mock LLM provider for integration tests                                       |\n   355→\n   356→---\n   357→\n   358→## 10. Environment & Secrets\n   359→\n   360→### Local development\n   361→\n   362→No secrets needed for unit/integration tests. E2E tests need:\n   363→\n   364→- `CLAUDE_CODE_OAUTH_TOKEN` — Claude Code access\n   365→- `GEMINI_API_KEY` — Gemini CLI access\n   366→- `HANKWEAVE_SENTINEL_ANTHROPIC_API_KEY` — for sentinel LLM calls\n   367→- Codex auth via `~/.codex/auth.json`\n   368→\n   369→### CI secrets\n   370→\n   371→Same as above, plus:\n   372→\n   373→- `NPM_TOKEN` — for npm publish\n   374→- `CODEX_AUTH_JSON` — Codex authentication JSON\n   375→- `PUBLIC_REPO_PAT` — GitHub PAT for pushing to the public repo during sync\n   376→\n   377→---\n   378→\n   379→## 11. AI Agent Policy\n   380→\n   381→AI agents (Claude, Codex, Cursor, or any other) operate under the following rules when working in this repo.\n   382→\n   383→### Permitted\n   384→\n   385→- Reading code, planning changes, writing code, running tests, running lint/typecheck\n   386→- Creating feature branches from develop\n   387→- Running `bun scripts/release.ts --dry-run` for validation\n   388→- Committing to feature branches under human supervision\n   389→- Opening pull requests targeting develop\n   390→\n   391→### Requires explicit human command and double verification\n   392→\n   393→- **Merging to `develop`** — an agent must never merge a PR or push directly to develop without a direct human instruction, and must confirm with the human before executing. No exceptions.\n   394→- **Merging to `release/alpha`** — same rule. The release script handles this as part of the release flow, but an agent must never run it without explicit human direction and must confirm the dry-run output with the human first.\n   395→- **Running the release script without `--dry-run`** — agents may run `--dry-run` freely, but executing a real release (`bun scripts/release.ts <bump>` or `bun scripts/release.ts <bump> --yes`) requires explicit human instruction.\n   396→- **Force pushing any branch** — must be explicitly requested and confirmed.\n   397→\n   398→### Forbidden (even if a human asks)\n   399→\n   400→- **Running `scripts/sync-public.ts` or `scripts/test-sync-local.ts --push`** — agents must never execute the sync to the public repo. If a sync is needed, ask the human to run it themselves. The dry-run variant (`test-sync-local.ts` without `--push`) is permitted.\n   401→- **Triggering the \"Sync to Public\" GitHub Actions workflow** — same rule. Never trigger it via `gh workflow run` or any other mechanism.\n   402→\n   403→### Rationale\n   404→\n   405→The public repo is the face of the project. A bad sync is visible to everyone and difficult to undo (npm publishes are permanent). The release script has rollback mechanisms for the private repo, but the public sync is a one-way door. Humans run it, humans own it.\n   406→\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"6f6abf3a-0b5f-4251-a578-7f2d66ac9b9a","uuid":"3b57d859-c6c8-475c-8fd8-72a23e99a33c","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/philip/projects/hw-budget-tests/plan-gen-execution/agentRoot/source/hankweave/standard-operating-procedures.md","content":"# Standard Operating Procedures\n\nThis document captures the development conventions, release workflow, and operational norms for the Hankweave codebase. Written for humans and AI agents working in the repo.\n\nLast updated: 2026-02-14 (v0.4.1).\n\n---\n\n## 1. Repository Structure\n\n```\nhankweave-dev/              ← private repo (SouthBridgeAI/hankweave)\n├── server/                 ← runtime source code (TypeScript, Bun)\n├── scripts/                ← build, release, sync, and dev tooling\n├── tests/                  ← unit, integration, e2e, cross-runtime\n├── schemas/                ← generated JSON schemas (from Zod)\n├── shims/                  ← agent harness adapters\n├── learning/               ← example hanks and guides (ships to public)\n├── intermediates/          ← planning docs, numbered by feature (internal only)\n├── external-docs/          ← SDK reference docs for agent context (internal only)\n├── public-release-files/   ← files overlaid onto the public repo during sync\n├── assets/                 ← images, demo GIFs\n├── .github/                ← CI workflows and composite actions\n├── CHANGELOG.md            ← Keep a Changelog format, [Unreleased] workflow\n├── CONTRIBUTING.md         ← branch model and PR guidelines (internal only)\n└── package.json            ← @southbridgeai/hankweave (private npm)\n```\n\nThere is a separate public repo at `SouthBridgeAI/hankweave-runtime` which receives sanitized snapshots via the sync workflow. It publishes the public `hankweave` npm package.\n\n### What gets stripped before public release\n\nThe sync process (`scripts/sync-public.ts`) removes internal-only content:\n\n- **Directories**: `intermediates/`, `external-docs/`, `public-release-files/`, `.github/actions/`\n- **Files**: `CLAUDE.md`, `CONTRIBUTING.md`, `PUBLISHING-PLAN.md`, `telemetry.md` (root), `reference/telemetry.md` (internal version), `.github/workflows/ci.yml`, `.github/workflows/sync-public.yml`, `scripts/sync-public.ts`, `scripts/test-sync-local.ts`, `scripts/release.ts`, `.DS_Store`\n\nThe `public-release-files/` directory contains replacements that get overlaid: a public-facing `README.md`, `LICENSE.md`, `NOTICE.md`, a sanitized `reference/telemetry.md`, and a simplified `.github/workflows/release.yml`.\n\nThe `package.json` is transformed: name becomes `hankweave`, `publishConfig.access` becomes `public`, `homepage` points to the public repo, and `release:*` scripts are removed.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Branch Model\n\n```\nfeature branches → develop → release/alpha\n                      ↑\n              all PRs target here\n```\n\n- **`develop`** — active development. All PRs target this branch. All work starts here.\n- **`release/alpha`** — the default branch on GitHub. Customer-facing. Tagged releases are cut from here. Never commit directly to release/alpha; it receives merges from develop only during the release process.\n- **No `main` or `master` branch.** This is intentional.\n\n### Feature branch naming\n\n- Author-prefixed: `hrishi/sentinel-qol-fixes`, `hrishi/init-wizard`\n- Descriptive: `fix/loop-iteration-telemetry`, `shim-timeout`\n- Ticket-based: `ENG-90`\n\nThe `hrishi/*` prefix is Hrishi's convention for easier identification. Philip uses bare names. Either is fine.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Development Workflow\n\n### The four-eyes principle\n\nWe operate on a **four-eyes principle** (ideally six, counting agents). The workflow for non-trivial changes:\n\n1. **Plan** — discuss the approach, often with an AI agent. Write a plan in `intermediates/<number>-<name>/plan.md` for larger features.\n2. **Review the plan** — read it critically. Replan if needed.\n3. **Implement** — have an agent write the code, or write it yourself.\n4. **Lint and test locally** — `bun run lint`, `bun run tc`, then tests (see testing order below).\n5. **Review the code line by line** — the author reads every line before pushing.\n6. **Push as a PR** — target `develop`. Include ticket numbers in the title.\n7. **Peer review** — another team member reviews before merging (~90% of the time, depending on size and risk).\n8. **Squash merge** — standard merge method. Keeps develop history clean.\n\n**Less code is more.** Hankweave has concurrent components and is deployed across multiple environments. Every line should be considered for its implications. Get as much reviewed locally before opening the PR.\n\nALL changes have at least two pairs of eyes reviewing them, and have enough deliberation. Often a review is requested from whoever is the implicit owner or originator of a section.\n\n### Intermediates\n\nThe `intermediates/` directory holds planning documents for features and investigations. Numbered sequentially (not tied to Linear tickets). Structure varies:\n\n- Single file: `intermediates/7-cleanup-integration-improvements.md`\n- Multi-phase: `intermediates/12-execution-isolation/1-plan.md` → `2-test-fixes.md` → `3-start-new.md` → `4-completed.md`\n- Bug reports: `intermediates/39-QoL-3/BUG_REPORT_HEADLESS_HTTP_CRASH.md`\n\nThese are stripped from release/alpha and the public repo.\n\n### External docs\n\n`external-docs/` holds SDK documentation and reference material (Claude Code SDK, AI SDK, Gemini CLI, etc.). These exist primarily so AI agents have context when working on integrations. Stripped before release.\n\n---\n\n## 4. PR Conventions\n\n### Title format\n\nInclude Linear ticket numbers when applicable:\n\n```\nFixes: Sentinel drains, telemetry fixes, rollback QoL (ENG-194, ENG-193)\nQuality-of-Life Improvements Batch 3 (ENG-158, ENG-180, ENG-179)\nCC not exiting Bug (ENG-186)\n```\n\nLinear is connected to GitHub — including the ticket number in the PR title updates the issue status when the PR merges.\n\n### Body format\n\nPR bodies are detailed. For each change, include:\n\n- **Problem** — what was wrong or what was needed\n- **Fix/Approach** — what was done and why\n- **Files** — which files were touched\n\nBatched PRs (multiple related changes) number each change. See PR #110 for the canonical example (22 numbered sections covering sentinel fixes, telemetry, TUI improvements, docs, and tests).\n\n### Changelog entries\n\nChangelog entries are typically **assembled in the PR body first**, then cleaned up or redacted at release time. The format is:\n\n```markdown\n- **Bold feature/fix name** — Description covering the what and why in one sentence.\n```\n\nExamples from the codebase:\n\n```markdown\n- **Sentinels watching `codon.completed` now reliably fire** — Added a second sentinel queue drain after the `codon.completed` event emission.\n- **Remote hank cache: hash-based freshness** — Branch caches now validate via `git ls-remote` instead of a 1-hour TTL.\n```\n\n### Labels and milestones\n\nNot actively used. Tracking lives in Linear.\n\n### Merge method\n\n**Squash merge** is the standard. This collapses the branch into a single commit on develop with the PR title as the message.\n\nRebasing is used when working on sequential changes on the same branch. Force-push to a feature branch is okay if you're sure nobody else is on it. Otherwise, cross-merge.\n\n### Attribution and accountability\n\n- **Humans are responsible for all activity.** Nothing is committed or written that hasn't been read end-to-end by a human. Agents operate under their human's supervision and responsibility.\n- **Always quote AI agents.** If a PR body, plan, or document was written by an agent, say so (e.g., \"generated by Opus 4.6, reviewed by @hrishioa\"). If an agent produced a code change, the PR should note which parts were agent-generated. See PR #113 for an example.\n- **Do not add AI tools as committers** in git commits. The human who reviewed and approved the work is the author.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Code Style\n\n- **Language**: TypeScript, ES2022 target, strict mode\n- **Runtime**: Bun (for execution, package management, and testing)\n- **Formatter/Linter**: Biome 2.x — 2-space indent, 100-char line width, LF endings\n  - Covers: `server/`, `tests/e2e/`, `tests/utils/`, `tests/unit/`\n  - Does not cover: `scripts/`, `tests/integration/`, `learning/`\n- **Line endings**: LF everywhere (enforced by `.gitattributes`)\n- **Type checking**: `bun run tc` (runs `tsc --noEmit`)\n\nRun before pushing:\n\n```bash\nbun run lint      # biome check\nbun run tc        # typecheck\n```\n\n---\n\n## 6. Testing\n\n### Test hierarchy (run in this order)\n\n1. **Unit tests** — `bun run test:unit` — fast, no external deps, mock-based\n2. **Integration tests** — `bun run test:integration` — component interactions, mock LLM providers, no API keys needed\n3. **E2E init tests** — `bun run test:e2e:init` — tests the `--init` scaffolding flow (needs real harness binaries)\n4. **Full E2E** — `bun run test` — includes happy-path and rollback tests (needs API keys: Claude, Gemini, Codex)\n\nTests can be expensive (real LLM calls in E2E). Start with unit, widen as confidence is needed.\n\n### CI matrix\n\nCI (`.github/workflows/ci.yml`) runs on every push to `develop` and PRs targeting it:\n\n- **Lint + typecheck** on ubuntu\n- **Unit + integration** on ubuntu, windows, macos\n- **Init E2E** (normal, npx, binary modes) on ubuntu, windows, macos\n- Uses composite actions in `.github/actions/` for environment setup, test execution, and log dumping on failure\n- E2E tests have retry logic (3 attempts, 10s between retries) because harness processes can be flaky\n\n### Test area\n\nTests use `tests/test-area/` for temporary files and `tests/test-results/` for output. Both are gitignored.\n\n---\n\n## 7. Release Process\n\n### Scripts\n\n| Script                        | Purpose                                                          |\n| ----------------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `scripts/release.ts`          | Version bump, changelog stamp, merge to release/alpha, tag, push |\n| `scripts/sync-public.ts`      | Snapshot private repo → transform → push to public repo          |\n| `scripts/test-sync-local.ts`  | Local dry-run of the sync process                                |\n| `scripts/build.ts`            | Build the npm package (dist/)                                    |\n| `scripts/build-executable.ts` | Build standalone Bun binaries for 5 platforms                    |\n| `scripts/generate-schemas.ts` | Generate JSON schemas from Zod types                             |\n\n### Release flow (private → npm + binaries)\n\n```bash\n# 1. Dry-run validation (recommended before every release)\nbun scripts/release.ts patch --dry-run\n\n# 2. Interactive release\nbun scripts/release.ts minor\n\n# 3. Headless release (for agents)\nbun scripts/release.ts patch --yes\n```\n\nThe release script has two phases:\n\n**Phase 1 — Validation** (no changes made):\n\n- Checks branch (must be develop), clean tree, remote sync\n- Computes new version, checks tag availability\n- Validates CHANGELOG has entries in [Unreleased]\n- Tests that develop → release/alpha merge would succeed (no conflicts)\n- Tests schema generation works\n- Runs typecheck and lint (skip with `--skip-checks`)\n- Prints a plan summary\n\n**Phase 2 — Execute** (with automatic rollback on failure):\n\n1. Bump version in package.json\n2. Stamp [Unreleased] → [version] - date in CHANGELOG\n3. Regenerate JSON schemas\n4. Commit \"Release vX.Y.Z\" on develop\n5. Add empty [Unreleased] template, commit\n6. Push develop\n7. Checkout release/alpha, merge develop, strip internal files (intermediates/, external-docs/, CLAUDE.md, CONTRIBUTING.md)\n8. Create tag on release/alpha\n9. Push release/alpha + tag (triggers CI)\n10. Return to develop\n\nIf any step fails after changes begin, the script attempts automatic rollback (reset branches to saved checkpoints, delete tags). If changes already reached remote, it prints manual recovery commands.\n\n### What CI does on tag push (private repo)\n\n`.github/workflows/release.yml` triggers on `v*` tags:\n\n1. **publish-npm** — typecheck, lint, tests, then `npm publish` as `@southbridgeai/hankweave` (restricted)\n2. **build-executables** — builds Bun binaries for linux-x64, linux-arm64, darwin-x64, darwin-arm64, windows-x64, with smoke tests on testable platforms\n3. **create-release** — downloads artifacts, creates GitHub release with binaries and changelog-derived release notes\n\n### Sync to public\n\nAfter the private release CI passes, manually trigger the \"Sync to Public\" workflow on GitHub:\n\n```\nActions → Sync to Public → Run workflow → version: 0.4.1\n```\n\nOr test locally first:\n\n```bash\n# Dry run (no push)\nbun scripts/test-sync-local.ts --version 0.4.1\n\n# Actually push\nbun scripts/test-sync-local.ts --version 0.4.1 --push\n```\n\nThe sync:\n\n1. Checks out the private repo at the version tag\n2. Clones the public repo's release/alpha branch\n3. Builds a snapshot: rsync (excluding internal files) → overlay public-release-files → remove ci.yml → transform package.json\n4. Extracts changelog (aggregates all versions since last public release)\n5. Replaces the public repo's tree with the snapshot\n6. Commits, tags, pushes to public repo\n\nThe public repo's tag push triggers its own `release.yml`: typecheck, lint, build, `npm publish --access public` as `hankweave`, build binaries, create GitHub release.\n\n### Version guidance\n\n- **patch** — bug fixes, docs, minor improvements\n- **minor** — new features, behavioral changes, UX overhauls\n- **major** — breaking changes (hasn't happened yet, pre-1.0)\n\n---\n\n## 8. CHANGELOG Conventions\n\nFormat follows [Keep a Changelog](https://keepachangelog.com/). Structure:\n\n```markdown\n## [Unreleased]\n\n### Added\n\n- **Feature name** — Description.\n\n### Changed\n\n- **Change name** — Description.\n\n### Fixed\n\n- **Fix name** — Description.\n\n## [0.4.1] - 2026-02-13\n\n...\n```\n\nRules:\n\n- Always have an `[Unreleased]` section at the top\n- Entries use **bold name** — description format\n- Group entries under Added/Changed/Fixed subsections\n- The release script stamps [Unreleased] → [version] - date automatically\n- Entries are assembled during PR work, cleaned up at release time\n- If a version wasn't synced to public, it can be consolidated (e.g., v0.4.0 content was rolled into v0.4.1 because v0.4.0 was internal-only)\n\n---\n\n## 9. Key Files Reference\n\n| File                                   | Purpose                                                                       |\n| -------------------------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `server/index.ts`                      | CLI entry point — arg parsing, routing to commands                            |\n| `server/hankweave-runtime.ts`          | Core runtime — codon execution, state management, event emission              |\n| `server/config.ts`                     | 5-layer config resolution (defaults → hankweave.json → hank.json → env → CLI) |\n| `server/basic-tui.ts`                  | Built-in terminal UI (WebSocket client)                                       |\n| `server/codon-runner.ts`               | Runs individual codons via harness managers                                   |\n| `server/claude-agent-sdk-manager.ts`   | Claude Code SDK integration                                                   |\n| `server/shim-process-manager.ts`       | Process manager for shim-based harnesses (Codex, Gemini)                      |\n| `server/remote-hank.ts`                | Git-based remote hank fetching and caching                                    |\n| `server/sentinels/`                    | Sentinel system (event-triggered observers)                                   |\n| `server/telemetry/`                    | Anonymous usage telemetry (PostHog)                                           |\n| `server/schemas/event-schemas.ts`      | WebSocket event type definitions (Zod)                                        |\n| `server/config-validation/`            | Validation schemas for hank files and configs                                 |\n| `tests/utils/sentinel-test-harness.ts` | Shared test utilities for sentinel tests                                      |\n| `tests/utils/mock-llm.ts`              | Mock LLM provider for integration tests                                       |\n\n---\n\n## 10. Environment & Secrets\n\n### Local development\n\nNo secrets needed for unit/integration tests. E2E tests need:\n\n- `CLAUDE_CODE_OAUTH_TOKEN` — Claude Code access\n- `GEMINI_API_KEY` — Gemini CLI access\n- `HANKWEAVE_SENTINEL_ANTHROPIC_API_KEY` — for sentinel LLM calls\n- Codex auth via `~/.codex/auth.json`\n\n### CI secrets\n\nSame as above, plus:\n\n- `NPM_TOKEN` — for npm publish\n- `CODEX_AUTH_JSON` — Codex authentication JSON\n- `PUBLIC_REPO_PAT` — GitHub PAT for pushing to the public repo during sync\n\n---\n\n## 11. AI Agent Policy\n\nAI agents (Claude, Codex, Cursor, or any other) operate under the following rules when working in this repo.\n\n### Permitted\n\n- Reading code, planning changes, writing code, running tests, running lint/typecheck\n- Creating feature branches from develop\n- Running `bun scripts/release.ts --dry-run` for validation\n- Committing to feature branches under human supervision\n- Opening pull requests targeting develop\n\n### Requires explicit human command and double verification\n\n- **Merging to `develop`** — an agent must never merge a PR or push directly to develop without a direct human instruction, and must confirm with the human before executing. No exceptions.\n- **Merging to `release/alpha`** — same rule. The release script handles this as part of the release flow, but an agent must never run it without explicit human direction and must confirm the dry-run output with the human first.\n- **Running the release script without `--dry-run`** — agents may run `--dry-run` freely, but executing a real release (`bun scripts/release.ts <bump>` or `bun scripts/release.ts <bump> --yes`) requires explicit human instruction.\n- **Force pushing any branch** — must be explicitly requested and confirmed.\n\n### Forbidden (even if a human asks)\n\n- **Running `scripts/sync-public.ts` or `scripts/test-sync-local.ts --push`** — agents must never execute the sync to the public repo. If a sync is needed, ask the human to run it themselves. The dry-run variant (`test-sync-local.ts` without `--push`) is permitted.\n- **Triggering the \"Sync to Public\" GitHub Actions workflow** — same rule. Never trigger it via `gh workflow run` or any other mechanism.\n\n### Rationale\n\nThe public repo is the face of the project. A bad sync is visible to everyone and difficult to undo (npm publishes are permanent). The release script has rollback mechanisms for the private repo, but the public sync is a one-way door. Humans run it, humans own it.\n","numLines":406,"startLine":1,"totalLines":406}},"timestamp":"2026-03-05T12:12:35.330Z"}
