{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01C2FvRa2ML3wUAi3PCkdugb","type":"tool_result","content":"     1→# Planning Log: Hankweave Monorepo Migration\n     2→\n     3→## Initial Observations from Task\n     4→\n     5→- Task is to migrate from multi-repo to monorepo (hankweave runtime + hankweave-docs)\n     6→- There's already a cross-repo dependency via `generate-schemas.ts` that hardcodes sibling paths\n     7→- Three repos involved: private dev (hankweave), docs (hankweave-docs), public mirror (hankweave-runtime)\n     8→- Key open questions: repo structure, public release pipeline, CI changes, Vercel config\n     9→- Bun workspaces is the natural fit since project already uses Bun\n    10→\n    11→## Areas to Read\n    12→\n    13→- [ ] source/hankweave/server - core runtime code\n    14→- [ ] source/hankweave/package.json - dependencies, scripts\n    15→- [ ] source/hankweave/scripts/ - build, release, sync, schema generation\n    16→- [ ] source/hankweave/.github/workflows/ - CI/CD\n    17→- [ ] source/hankweave/biome.json - linting config\n    18→- [ ] source/hankweave/bunfig.toml - bun config\n    19→- [ ] source/hankweave/tsconfig.json - typescript config\n    20→- [ ] source/hankweave/standard-operating-procedures.md - conventions\n    21→- [ ] source/hankweave-docs/ - docs site structure\n    22→- [ ] source/hankweave/tests/ - test infrastructure\n    23→\n    24→## Questions\n    25→\n    26→1. What is the full directory structure of hankweave?\n    27→2. How are scripts invoked (bun run, package.json scripts)?\n    28→3. What does the sync-public script strip?\n    29→4. What's the test infrastructure like?\n    30→5. How deep is the server/ directory tree?\n    31→6. What does the docs site structure look like?\n    32→\n    33→## Aha Moments\n    34→\n    35→(to be filled as I read)\n    36→\n    37→## Tasks\n    38→\n    39→- Read through all source code systematically\n    40→- Understand conventions and patterns\n    41→- Draft plan\n    42→- Review tests\n    43→- Review docs\n    44→- Write addendums\n    45→\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"6f6abf3a-0b5f-4251-a578-7f2d66ac9b9a","uuid":"2c584e8c-5826-4108-94bf-72c26aff6bf7","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/philip/projects/hw-budget-tests/plan-gen-execution/agentRoot/plan/rough-workspace/opus/log.md","content":"# Planning Log: Hankweave Monorepo Migration\n\n## Initial Observations from Task\n\n- Task is to migrate from multi-repo to monorepo (hankweave runtime + hankweave-docs)\n- There's already a cross-repo dependency via `generate-schemas.ts` that hardcodes sibling paths\n- Three repos involved: private dev (hankweave), docs (hankweave-docs), public mirror (hankweave-runtime)\n- Key open questions: repo structure, public release pipeline, CI changes, Vercel config\n- Bun workspaces is the natural fit since project already uses Bun\n\n## Areas to Read\n\n- [ ] source/hankweave/server - core runtime code\n- [ ] source/hankweave/package.json - dependencies, scripts\n- [ ] source/hankweave/scripts/ - build, release, sync, schema generation\n- [ ] source/hankweave/.github/workflows/ - CI/CD\n- [ ] source/hankweave/biome.json - linting config\n- [ ] source/hankweave/bunfig.toml - bun config\n- [ ] source/hankweave/tsconfig.json - typescript config\n- [ ] source/hankweave/standard-operating-procedures.md - conventions\n- [ ] source/hankweave-docs/ - docs site structure\n- [ ] source/hankweave/tests/ - test infrastructure\n\n## Questions\n\n1. What is the full directory structure of hankweave?\n2. How are scripts invoked (bun run, package.json scripts)?\n3. What does the sync-public script strip?\n4. What's the test infrastructure like?\n5. How deep is the server/ directory tree?\n6. What does the docs site structure look like?\n\n## Aha Moments\n\n(to be filled as I read)\n\n## Tasks\n\n- Read through all source code systematically\n- Understand conventions and patterns\n- Draft plan\n- Review tests\n- Review docs\n- Write addendums\n","numLines":45,"startLine":1,"totalLines":45}},"timestamp":"2026-03-05T12:14:09.232Z"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_0144BVCTc37XCDYLgTCbiPxY","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_014PY6946XbKjAJMvGcvLwGD","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/Users/philip/projects/hw-budget-tests/plan-gen-execution/agentRoot/plan/rough-workspace/opus/log.md","content":"# Planning Log: Hankweave Monorepo Migration\n\n## Areas Read\n\n- [x] source/hankweave/server - core runtime code (60+ .ts files, well-organized subdirs)\n- [x] source/hankweave/package.json - @southbridgeai/hankweave v0.5.6, Bun runtime, many scripts\n- [x] source/hankweave/scripts/ - build.ts, build-executable.ts, release.ts, sync-public.ts, generate-schemas.ts, e2e/init.ts, run-cross-runtime-tests.ts, test-sync-local.ts\n- [x] source/hankweave/.github/workflows/ - ci.yml, release.yml, sync-public.yml\n- [x] source/hankweave/.github/actions/ - setup-environment, run-tests, dump-test-logs (composite actions)\n- [x] source/hankweave/biome.json - covers server/, tests/e2e/, tests/utils/, tests/unit/, types/\n- [x] source/hankweave/bunfig.toml - minimal, just BUN_TEST env var for tests\n- [x] source/hankweave/tsconfig.json - ES2022, strict, bun-types\n- [x] source/hankweave/.gitignore, .npmignore, .gitattributes\n- [x] source/hankweave/standard-operating-procedures.md - comprehensive SOPs\n- [x] source/hankweave-docs/ - Nextra 4, Next.js 15, Bun install+build\n- [x] source/hankweave-docs/nextra-site/package.json - hankweave-docs v2.0.0\n- [x] source/hankweave-docs/nextra-site/vercel.json - framework: nextjs, bun install/build\n- [x] source/hankweave-docs/nextra-site/tsconfig.json - ES2020, strict:false, bundler resolution\n- [x] source/hankweave/tests/ - 65+ unit tests, 10+ integration, 10+ e2e, cross-runtime tests\n- [x] plan/background/background.md - pre-compiled research\n\n## Aha Moments\n\n### 1. The docs project has a nested structure\nThe docs repo is `hankweave-docs/` at root but the actual Next.js project is at `hankweave-docs/nextra-site/`. The `package.json`, `tsconfig.json`, `next.config.mjs` etc. are all inside `nextra-site/`. The docs repo root has `README.md`, `.gitignore`, `notes/`, `plan/`.\n\nThis means that wherever docs lands in the monorepo, you need to decide: does the workspace point to the `nextra-site/` subdirectory (where package.json lives), or to the outer `hankweave-docs/` directory?\n\n**Decision**: The workspace should point to where `package.json` is — so the `nextra-site/` level. The outer `hankweave-docs/` scaffold (README, notes, plan) is development overhead that probably doesn't need its own workspace and can either be flattened or kept as-is.\n\n### 2. generate-schemas.ts is the only real cross-repo code reference\nGrep confirms only `scripts/generate-schemas.ts:28` references `hankweave-docs` in actual code. All other references are in `intermediates/` planning documents (which are stripped on release). This simplifies the migration — only one file needs a path adjustment.\n\n### 3. The docs have NO dependency on the runtime package\nThe docs site (`hankweave-docs`) doesn't `import` anything from `@southbridgeai/hankweave`. The only cross-repo data flow is schemas generated by the runtime and written to the docs `public/schemas/` directory. A `workspace:*` dependency is NOT needed at this time.\n\n### 4. The public sync pipeline only needs to export the runtime\nThe `sync-public.ts` script works from the root of the hankweave repo. In a monorepo, if the runtime stays at the root, the sync script barely changes. If it moves into a subdirectory, the strip lists and snapshot process need reworking.\n\n### 5. CI uses composite actions with relative paths\nAll three composite actions (`.github/actions/*`) are referenced as `./.github/actions/setup-environment` etc. These use relative paths. As long as the `.github/` directory stays at the monorepo root, they work. The composite actions themselves just do `bun install --frozen-lockfile` and `bun test` — they'd work with workspaces too.\n\n### 6. The release script assumes it runs from the repo root\n`release.ts` uses `resolve(process.cwd())` for paths and runs `git` commands. It also runs `bun run generate-schemas` and `bun run tc`. These all assume the cwd is the package root. In a monorepo with the runtime as a workspace, scripts would need to be run from the workspace directory or paths adjusted.\n\n### 7. build.ts uses relative paths from import.meta.dir\n`build.ts` resolves `distDir` as `join(import.meta.dir, \"..\", \"dist\")` and entry point as `\"./server/index.ts\"`. This means build.ts must run from (or be relative to) the runtime package root. Same for `build-executable.ts`.\n\n### 8. Vercel monorepo support just needs \"Root Directory\"\nVercel lets you set the \"Root Directory\" for a project in project settings. The current `vercel.json` in `nextra-site/` would work if Vercel is pointed at the right subdirectory. This is a settings change, not a code change.\n\n### 9. The docs package.json has a `postbuild` step\n`\"postbuild\": \"pagefind --site .next/server/app --output-path public/_pagefind\"` — this runs Pagefind after `next build`. This would work fine in a workspace since `next build` runs from the workspace directory.\n\n### 10. tsconfig.json settings differ significantly between the two projects\n- Runtime: `target: ES2022`, `module: ES2022`, `moduleResolution: node`, `strict: true`, types: `bun-types`\n- Docs: `target: ES2020`, `module: esnext`, `moduleResolution: bundler`, `strict: false`, plugins: `next`\n\nThese configs should NOT be shared. Each workspace needs its own `tsconfig.json`.\n\n### 11. There's only one bun.lock file per project\nCurrently `source/hankweave/bun.lock` and `source/hankweave-docs/nextra-site/bun.lock`. In a monorepo, there would be a single root-level `bun.lock` file managed by Bun workspaces.\n\n### 12. The runtime's `.npmignore` strips `server/`, `scripts/`, `tests/` for npm publish\nnpm publish only includes `dist/`, `shims/`, `schemas/`. This is controlled by both `.npmignore` and the `\"files\"` field in `package.json`. In a monorepo, the npm publish command would need to run from the runtime workspace. `\"files\"` field should continue to work correctly.\n\n## Key Decision Points\n\n### Structure Decision: Runtime at root vs in a subdirectory\n\n**Option A: Runtime stays at repo root, docs moves in as a workspace**\n```\nhankweave/                  (monorepo root = runtime package root)\n├── package.json            (root package.json with workspaces field, ALSO the runtime)\n├── server/\n├── scripts/\n├── tests/\n├── docs/                   (was: hankweave-docs/nextra-site)\n│   ├── package.json\n│   ├── app/\n│   ├── content/\n│   └── ...\n└── ...\n```\n- Pro: Minimal disruption to existing paths, scripts, CI\n- Pro: All existing relative paths in server/scripts still work\n- Con: The root package.json serves dual purpose (workspace root AND runtime package)\n- Con: `bun install` at root installs both workspace deps — might be slightly confusing\n\n**Option B: Both packages in subdirectories**\n```\nhankweave/                  (monorepo root)\n├── package.json            (workspace root, not a package itself)\n├── packages/\n│   ├── runtime/            (was: hankweave root)\n│   │   ├── package.json\n│   │   ├── server/\n│   │   └── ...\n│   └── docs/               (was: hankweave-docs/nextra-site)\n│       ├── package.json\n│       └── ...\n└── .github/\n```\n- Pro: Clean separation, conventional monorepo structure\n- Con: MASSIVE amount of path changes — every script, CI workflow, build config, `import.meta.dir` reference\n- Con: git history gets complicated\n- Con: The release.ts, sync-public.ts, build.ts all need significant reworking\n\n**Option C: Runtime at root, docs as apps/docs**\n```\nhankweave/                  (monorepo root = runtime package root)\n├── package.json            (root + runtime)\n├── server/\n├── apps/\n│   └── docs/               (was: hankweave-docs/nextra-site)\n│       ├── package.json\n│       └── ...\n└── ...\n```\n- Pro: Distinguishes \"apps\" (deployable) from packages (publishable) — common Turborepo pattern\n- Con: `apps/` is a Turborepo convention that might be overengineering for two packages\n- Otherwise similar to Option A\n\n### Recommendation: Option A (runtime at root)\n\nThe path of least disruption. The runtime is the primary package — it has the most scripts, CI workflows, and tooling built around its position at root. Moving it into a subdirectory would require changing dozens of paths across scripts, workflows, and configs.\n\nThe docs site is the smaller, simpler addition. Putting it at `docs/` (or `docs/nextra-site/`) within the runtime's repo structure is the minimal change.\n\nThe trade-off: the root `package.json` serves double duty. But Bun workspaces handles this fine — you can have `\"workspaces\": [\"docs\"]` in the root and the root itself is also a package.\n\n### Sync-public impact\n\nWith Option A (runtime at root), the sync-public.ts barely changes. The only new item is that the `docs/` directory needs to be added to the `STRIP_DIRS` list so it doesn't end up in the public runtime snapshot. Everything else stays the same.\n\n### CI impact\n\nWith Option A:\n- `ci.yml` barely changes — the runtime is still at root, `bun install` installs all workspaces, tests run from root\n- `release.yml` same thing\n- Optional: Add path filtering so docs-only changes skip runtime tests (nice to have, not required initially)\n\n### generate-schemas.ts impact\n\nThe `DOCS_SCHEMA_DIR` path changes from `../../hankweave-docs/nextra-site/public/schemas` to `../docs/public/schemas` (one level up from `scripts/`, then into `docs/`). This is actually simpler.\n\n## Questions Resolved\n\n1. **Full directory structure** — mapped exhaustively\n2. **Script invocation** — all via `bun run <script>` or `bun scripts/<file>.ts`\n3. **Sync-public strips** — STRIP_DIRS + STRIP_FILES documented, understood\n4. **Test infrastructure** — bun:test, 65+ unit, 10+ integration, e2e with mock LLMs\n5. **server/ depth** — 3-4 levels (server/sentinels/, server/llm/, server/types/, etc.)\n6. **Docs structure** — Nextra 4 + Next.js 15, content/ for MDX, app/ for routes, components/ for custom\n\n## Questions Remaining\n\n1. **Should the outer hankweave-docs/ wrapper be kept?** — The docs repo has a root-level README, notes/, plan/ directory outside nextra-site/. These are dev-only files. Probably flatten: just bring nextra-site/ contents into docs/.\n2. **Shared dev dependencies?** — Both projects use TypeScript. The docs use `@types/node` and `@types/react`. The runtime uses `@types/bun`. Bun catalogs could share TypeScript version, but it's not critical.\n3. **Does the monorepo need its own README at root?** — The current runtime README is internal. The public one is in `public-release-files/`. Adding a monorepo root README could be helpful but isn't strictly necessary for the migration.\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":811,"cache_read_input_tokens":126776,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":811},"output_tokens":3200,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"6f6abf3a-0b5f-4251-a578-7f2d66ac9b9a","uuid":"f1de8154-b1db-412f-8a40-0f2e03b048d4","timestamp":"2026-03-05T12:15:22.330Z"}
