github/actions/*`) are referenced as `./.github/actions/setup-environment` etc. These use relative paths. As long as the `.github/` directory stays at the monorepo root, they work. The composite actions themselves just do `bun install --frozen-lockfile` and `bun test` — they'd work with workspaces too.\n    42→\n    43→### 6. The release script assumes it runs from the repo root\n    44→`release.ts` uses `resolve(process.cwd())` for paths and runs `git` commands. It also runs `bun run generate-schemas` and `bun run tc`. These all assume the cwd is the package root. In a monorepo with the runtime as a workspace, scripts would need to be run from the workspace directory or paths adjusted.\n    45→\n    46→### 7. build.ts uses relative paths from import.meta.dir\n    47→`build.ts` resolves `distDir` as `join(import.meta.dir, \"..\", \"dist\")` and entry point as `\"./server/index.ts\"`. This means build.ts must run from (or be relative to) the runtime package root. Same for `build-executable.ts`.\n    48→\n    49→### 8. Vercel monorepo support just needs \"Root Directory\"\n    50→Vercel lets you set the \"Root Directory\" for a project in project settings. The current `vercel.json` in `nextra-site/` would work if Vercel is pointed at the right subdirectory. This is a settings change, not a code change.\n    51→\n    52→### 9. The docs package.json has a `postbuild` step\n    53→`\"postbuild\": \"pagefind --site .next/server/app --output-path public/_pagefind\"` — this runs Pagefind after `next build`. This would work fine in a workspace since `next build` runs from the workspace directory.\n    54→\n    55→### 10. tsconfig.json settings differ significantly between the two projects\n    56→- Runtime: `target: ES2022`, `module: ES2022`, `moduleResolution: node`, `strict: true`, types: `bun-types`\n    57→- Docs: `target: ES2020`, `module: esnext`, `moduleResolution: bundler`, `strict: false`, plugins: `next`\n    58→\n    59→These configs should NOT be shared. Each workspace needs its own `tsconfig.json`.\n    60→\n    61→### 11. There's only one bun.lock file per project\n    62→Currently `source/hankweave/bun.lock` and `source/hankweave-docs/nextra-site/bun.lock`. In a monorepo, there would be a single root-level `bun.lock` file managed by Bun workspaces.\n    63→\n    64→### 12. The runtime's `.npmignore` strips `server/`, `scripts/`, `tests/` for npm publish\n    65→npm publish only includes `dist/`, `shims/`, `schemas/`. This is controlled by both `.npmignore` and the `\"files\"` field in `package.json`. In a monorepo, the npm publish command would need to run from the runtime workspace. `\"files\"` field should continue to work correctly.\n    66→\n    67→## Key Decision Points\n    68→\n    69→### Structure Decision: Runtime at root vs in a subdirectory\n    70→\n    71→**Option A: Runtime stays at repo root, docs moves in as a workspace**\n    72→```\n    73→hankweave/                  (monorepo root = runtime package root)\n    74→├── package.json            (root package.json with workspaces field, ALSO the runtime)\n    75→├── server/\n    76→├── scripts/\n    77→├── tests/\n    78→├── docs/                   (was: hankweave-docs/nextra-site)\n    79→│   ├── package.json\n    80→│   ├── app/\n    81→│   ├── content/\n    82→│   └── ...\n    83→└── ...\n    84→```\n    85→- Pro: Minimal disruption to existing paths, scripts, CI\n    86→- Pro: All existing relative paths in server/scripts still work\n    87→- Con: The root package.json serves dual purpose (workspace root AND runtime package)\n    88→- Con: `bun install` at root installs both workspace deps — might be slightly confusing\n    89→\n    90→**Option B: Both packages in subdirectories**\n    91→```\n    92→hankweave/                  (monorepo root)\n    93→├── package.json            (workspace root, not a package itself)\n    94→├── packages/\n    95→│   ├── runtime/            (was: hankweave root)\n    96→│   │   ├── package.json\n    97→│   │   ├── server/\n    98→│   │   └── ...\n    99→│   └── docs/               (was: hankweave-docs/nextra-site)\n   100→│       ├── package.json\n   101→│       └── ...\n   102→└── .github/\n   103→```\n   104→- Pro: Clean separation, conventional monorepo structure\n   105→- Con: MASSIVE amount of path changes — every script, CI workflow, build config, `import.meta.dir` reference\n   106→- Con: git history gets complicated\n   107→- Con: The release.ts, sync-public.ts, build.ts all need significant reworking\n   108→\n   109→**Option C: Runtime at root, docs as apps/docs**\n   110→```\n   111→hankweave/                  (monorepo root = runtime package root)\n   112→├── package.json            (root + runtime)\n   113→├── server/\n   114→├── apps/\n   115→│   └── docs/               (was: hankweave-docs/nextra-site)\n   116→│       ├── package.json\n   117→│       └── ...\n   118→└── ...\n   119→```\n   120→- Pro: Distinguishes \"apps\" (deployable) from packages (publishable) — common Turborepo pattern\n   121→- Con: `apps/` is a Turborepo convention that might be overengineering for two packages\n   122→- Otherwise similar to Option A\n   123→\n   124→### Recommendation: Option A (runtime at root)\n   125→\n   126→The path of least disruption. The runtime is the primary package — it has the most scripts, CI workflows, and tooling built around its position at root. Moving it into a subdirectory would require changing dozens of paths across scripts, workflows, and configs.\n   127→\n   128→The docs site is the smaller, simpler addition. Putting it at `docs/` (or `docs/nextra-site/`) within the runtime's repo structure is the minimal change.\n   129→\n   130→The trade-off: the root `package.json` serves double duty. But Bun workspaces handles this fine — you can have `\"workspaces\": [\"docs\"]` in the root and the root itself is also a package.\n   131→\n   132→### Sync-public impact\n   133→\n   134→With Option A (runtime at root), the sync-public.ts barely changes. The only new item is that the `docs/` directory needs to be added to the `STRIP_DIRS` list so it doesn't end up in the public runtime snapshot. Everything else stays the same.\n   135→\n   136→### CI impact\n   137→\n   138→With Option A:\n   139→- `ci.yml` barely changes — the runtime is still at root, `bun install` installs all workspaces, tests run from root\n   140→- `release.yml` same thing\n   141→- Optional: Add path filtering so docs-only changes skip runtime tests (nice to have, not required initially)\n   142→\n   143→### generate-schemas.ts impact\n   144→\n   145→The `DOCS_SCHEMA_DIR` path changes from `../../hankweave-docs/nextra-site/public/schemas` to `../docs/public/schemas` (one level up from `scripts/`, then into `docs/`). This is actually simpler.\n   146→\n   147→## Questions Resolved\n   148→\n   149→1. **Full directory structure** — mapped exhaustively\n   150→2. **Script invocation** — all via `bun run <script>` or `bun scripts/<file>.ts`\n   151→3. **Sync-public strips** — STRIP_DIRS + STRIP_FILES documented, understood\n   152→4. **Test infrastructure** — bun:test, 65+ unit, 10+ integration, e2e with mock LLMs\n   153→5. **server/ depth** — 3-4 levels (server/sentinels/, server/llm/, server/types/, etc.)\n   154→6. **Docs structure** — Nextra 4 + Next.js 15, content/ for MDX, app/ for routes, components/ for custom\n   155→\n   156→## Questions Remaining\n   157→\n   158→1. **Should the outer hankweave-docs/ wrapper be kept?** — The docs repo has a root-level README, notes/, plan/ directory outside nextra-site/. These are dev-only files. Probably flatten: just bring nextra-site/ contents into docs/.\n   159→2. **Shared dev dependencies?** — Both projects use TypeScript. The docs use `@types/node` and `@types/react`. The runtime uses `@types/bun`. Bun catalogs could share TypeScript version, but it's not critical.\n   160→3. **Does the monorepo need its own README at root?** — The current runtime README is internal. The public one is in `public-release-files/`. Adding a monorepo root README could be helpful but isn't strictly necessary for the migration.\n   161→"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"6f6abf3a-0b5f-4251-a578-7f2d66ac9b9a","uuid":"b343e2f2-12a6-4509-9544-ca4bed58a1ff","tool_use_result":{"type":"update","filePath":"/Users/philip/projects/hw-budget-tests/plan-gen-execution/agentRoot/plan/rough-workspace/opus/log.md","content":"# Planning Log: Hankweave Monorepo Migration\n\n## Areas Read\n\n- [x] source/hankweave/server - core runtime code (60+ .ts files, well-organized subdirs)\n- [x] source/hankweave/package.json - @southbridgeai/hankweave v0.5.6, Bun runtime, many scripts\n- [x] source/hankweave/scripts/ - build.ts, build-executable.ts, release.ts, sync-public.ts, generate-schemas.ts, e2e/init.ts, run-cross-runtime-tests.ts, test-sync-local.ts\n- [x] source/hankweave/.github/workflows/ - ci.yml, release.yml, sync-public.yml\n- [x] source/hankweave/.github/actions/ - setup-environment, run-tests, dump-test-logs (composite actions)\n- [x] source/hankweave/biome.json - covers server/, tests/e2e/, tests/utils/, tests/unit/, types/\n- [x] source/hankweave/bunfig.toml - minimal, just BUN_TEST env var for tests\n- [x] source/hankweave/tsconfig.json - ES2022, strict, bun-types\n- [x] source/hankweave/.gitignore, .npmignore, .gitattributes\n- [x] source/hankweave/standard-operating-procedures.md - comprehensive SOPs\n- [x] source/hankweave-docs/ - Nextra 4, Next.js 15, Bun install+build\n- [x] source/hankweave-docs/nextra-site/package.json - hankweave-docs v2.0.0\n- [x] source/hankweave-docs/nextra-site/vercel.json - framework: nextjs, bun install/build\n- [x] source/hankweave-docs/nextra-site/tsconfig.json - ES2020, strict:false, bundler resolution\n- [x] source/hankweave/tests/ - 65+ unit tests, 10+ integration, 10+ e2e, cross-runtime tests\n- [x] plan/background/background.md - pre-compiled research\n\n## Aha Moments\n\n### 1. The docs project has a nested structure\nThe docs repo is `hankweave-docs/` at root but the actual Next.js project is at `hankweave-docs/nextra-site/`. The `package.json`, `tsconfig.json`, `next.config.mjs` etc. are all inside `nextra-site/`. The docs repo root has `README.md`, `.gitignore`, `notes/`, `plan/`.\n\nThis means that wherever docs lands in the monorepo, you need to decide: does the workspace point to the `nextra-site/` subdirectory (where package.json lives), or to the outer `hankweave-docs/` directory?\n\n**Decision**: The workspace should point to where `package.json` is — so the `nextra-site/` level. The outer `hankweave-docs/` scaffold (README, notes, plan) is development overhead that probably doesn't need its own workspace and can either be flattened or kept as-is.\n\n### 2. generate-schemas.ts is the only real cross-repo code reference\nGrep confirms only `scripts/generate-schemas.ts:28` references `hankweave-docs` in actual code. All other references are in `intermediates/` planning documents (which are stripped on release). This simplifies the migration — only one file needs a path adjustment.\n\n### 3. The docs have NO dependency on the runtime package\nThe docs site (`hankweave-docs`) doesn't `import` anything from `@southbridgeai/hankweave`. The only cross-repo data flow is schemas generated by the runtime and written to the docs `public/schemas/` directory. A `workspace:*` dependency is NOT needed at this time.\n\n### 4. The public sync pipeline only needs to export the runtime\nThe `sync-public.ts` script works from the root of the hankweave repo. In a monorepo, if the runtime stays at the root, the sync script barely changes. If it moves into a subdirectory, the strip lists and snapshot process need reworking.\n\n### 5. CI uses composite actions with relative paths\nAll three composite actions (`.github/actions/*`) are referenced as `./.github/actions/setup-environment` etc. These use relative paths. As long as the `.github/` directory stays at the monorepo root, they work. The composite actions themselves just do `bun install --frozen-lockfile` and `bun test` — they'd work with workspaces too.\n\n### 6. The release script assumes it runs from the repo root\n`release.ts` uses `resolve(process.cwd())` for paths and runs `git` commands. It also runs `bun run generate-schemas` and `bun run tc`. These all assume the cwd is the package root. In a monorepo with the runtime as a workspace, scripts would need to be run from the workspace directory or paths adjusted.\n\n### 7. build.ts uses relative paths from import.meta.dir\n`build.ts` resolves `distDir` as `join(import.meta.dir, \"..\", \"dist\")` and entry point as `\"./server/index.ts\"`. This means build.ts must run from (or be relative to) the runtime package root. Same for `build-executable.ts`.\n\n### 8. Vercel monorepo support just needs \"Root Directory\"\nVercel lets you set the \"Root Directory\" for a project in project settings. The current `vercel.json` in `nextra-site/` would work if Vercel is pointed at the right subdirectory. This is a settings change, not a code change.\n\n### 9. The docs package.json has a `postbuild` step\n`\"postbuild\": \"pagefind --site .next/server/app --output-path public/_pagefind\"` — this runs Pagefind after `next build`. This would work fine in a workspace since `next build` runs from the workspace directory.\n\n### 10. tsconfig.json settings differ significantly between the two projects\n- Runtime: `target: ES2022`, `module: ES2022`, `moduleResolution: node`, `strict: true`, types: `bun-types`\n- Docs: `target: ES2020`, `module: esnext`, `moduleResolution: bundler`, `strict: false`, plugins: `next`\n\nThese configs should NOT be shared. Each workspace needs its own `tsconfig.json`.\n\n### 11. There's only one bun.lock file per project\nCurrently `source/hankweave/bun.lock` and `source/hankweave-docs/nextra-site/bun.lock`. In a monorepo, there would be a single root-level `bun.lock` file managed by Bun workspaces.\n\n### 12. The runtime's `.npmignore` strips `server/`, `scripts/`, `tests/` for npm publish\nnpm publish only includes `dist/`, `shims/`, `schemas/`. This is controlled by both `.npmignore` and the `\"files\"` field in `package.json`. In a monorepo, the npm publish command would need to run from the runtime workspace. `\"files\"` field should continue to work correctly.\n\n## Key Decision Points\n\n### Structure Decision: Runtime at root vs in a subdirectory\n\n**Option A: Runtime stays at repo root, docs moves in as a workspace**\n```\nhankweave/                  (monorepo root = runtime package root)\n├── package.json            (root package.json with workspaces field, ALSO the runtime)\n├── server/\n├── scripts/\n├── tests/\n├── docs/                   (was: hankweave-docs/nextra-site)\n│   ├── package.json\n│   ├── app/\n│   ├── content/\n│   └── ...\n└── ...\n```\n- Pro: Minimal disruption to existing paths, scripts, CI\n- Pro: All existing relative paths in server/scripts still work\n- Con: The root package.json serves dual purpose (workspace root AND runtime package)\n- Con: `bun install` at root installs both workspace deps — might be slightly confusing\n\n**Option B: Both packages in subdirectories**\n```\nhankweave/                  (monorepo root)\n├── package.json            (workspace root, not a package itself)\n├── packages/\n│   ├── runtime/            (was: hankweave root)\n│   │   ├── package.json\n│   │   ├── server/\n│   │   └── ...\n│   └── docs/               (was: hankweave-docs/nextra-site)\n│       ├── package.json\n│       └── ...\n└── .github/\n```\n- Pro: Clean separation, conventional monorepo structure\n- Con: MASSIVE amount of path changes — every script, CI workflow, build config, `import.meta.dir` reference\n- Con: git history gets complicated\n- Con: The release.ts, sync-public.ts, build.ts all need significant reworking\n\n**Option C: Runtime at root, docs as apps/docs**\n```\nhankweave/                  (monorepo root = runtime package root)\n├── package.json            (root + runtime)\n├── server/\n├── apps/\n│   └── docs/               (was: hankweave-docs/nextra-site)\n│       ├── package.json\n│       └── ...\n└── ...\n```\n- Pro: Distinguishes \"apps\" (deployable) from packages (publishable) — common Turborepo pattern\n- Con: `apps/` is a Turborepo convention that might be overengineering for two packages\n- Otherwise similar to Option A\n\n### Recommendation: Option A (runtime at root)\n\nThe path of least disruption. The runtime is the primary package — it has the most scripts, CI workflows, and tooling built around its position at root. Moving it into a subdirectory would require changing dozens of paths across scripts, workflows, and configs.\n\nThe docs site is the smaller, simpler addition. Putting it at `docs/` (or `docs/nextra-site/`) within the runtime's repo structure is the minimal change.\n\nThe trade-off: the root `package.json` serves double duty. But Bun workspaces handles this fine — you can have `\"workspaces\": [\"docs\"]` in the root and the root itself is also a package.\n\n### Sync-public impact\n\nWith Option A (runtime at root), the sync-public.ts barely changes. The only new item is that the `docs/` directory needs to be added to the `STRIP_DIRS` list so it doesn't end up in the public runtime snapshot. Everything else stays the same.\n\n### CI impact\n\nWith Option A:\n- `ci.yml` barely changes — the runtime is still at root, `bun install` installs all workspaces, tests run from root\n- `release.yml` same thing\n- Optional: Add path filtering so docs-only changes skip runtime tests (nice to have, not required initially)\n\n### generate-schemas.ts impact\n\nThe `DOCS_SCHEMA_DIR` path changes from `../../hankweave-docs/nextra-site/public/schemas` to `../docs/public/schemas` (one level up from `scripts/`, then into `docs/`). This is actually simpler.\n\n## Questions Resolved\n\n1. **Full directory structure** — mapped exhaustively\n2. **Script invocation** — all via `bun run <script>` or `bun scripts/<file>.ts`\n3. **Sync-public strips** — STRIP_DIRS + STRIP_FILES documented, understood\n4. **Test infrastructure** — bun:test, 65+ unit, 10+ integration, e2e with mock LLMs\n5. **server/ depth** — 3-4 levels (server/sentinels/, server/llm/, server/types/, etc.)\n6. **Docs structure** — Nextra 4 + Next.js 15, content/ for MDX, app/ for routes, components/ for custom\n\n## Questions Remaining\n\n1. **Should the outer hankweave-docs/ wrapper be kept?** — The docs repo has a root-level README, notes/, plan/ directory outside nextra-site/. These are dev-only files. Probably flatten: just bring nextra-site/ contents into docs/.\n2. **Shared dev dependencies?** — Both projects use TypeScript. The docs use `@types/node` and `@types/react`. The runtime uses `@types/bun`. Bun catalogs could share TypeScript version, but it's not critical.\n3. **Does the monorepo need its own README at root?** — The current runtime README is internal. The public one is in `public-release-files/`. Adding a monorepo root README could be helpful but isn't strictly necessary for the migration.\n","structuredPatch":[{"oldStart":1,"oldLines":44,"newStart":1,"newLines":160,"lines":[" # Planning Log: Hankweave Monorepo Migration"," ","-## Initial Observations from Task","+## Areas Read"," ","-- Task is to migrate from multi-repo to monorepo (hankweave runtime + hankweave-docs)","-- There's already a cross-repo dependency via `generate-schemas.ts` that hardcodes sibling paths","-- Three repos involved: private dev (hankweave), docs (hankweave-docs), public mirror (hankweave-runtime)","-- Key open questions: repo structure, public release pipeline, CI changes, Vercel config","-- Bun workspaces is the natural fit since project already uses Bun","+- [x] source/hankweave/server - core runtime code (60+ .ts files, well-organized subdirs)","+- [x] source/hankweave/package.json - @southbridgeai/hankweave v0.5.6, Bun runtime, many scripts","+- [x] source/hankweave/scripts/ - build.ts, build-executable.ts, release.ts, sync-public.ts, generate-schemas.ts, e2e/init.ts, run-cross-runtime-tests.ts, test-sync-local.ts","+- [x] source/hankweave/.github/workflows/ - ci.yml, release.yml, sync-public.yml","+- [x] source/hankweave/.github/actions/ - setup-environment, run-tests, dump-test-logs (composite actions)","+- [x] source/hankweave/biome.json - covers server/, tests/e2e/, tests/utils/, tests/unit/, types/","+- [x] source/hankweave/bunfig.toml - minimal, just BUN_TEST env var for tests","+- [x] source/hankweave/tsconfig.json - ES2022, strict, bun-types","+- [x] source/hankweave/.gitignore, .npmignore, .gitattributes","+- [x] source/hankweave/standard-operating-procedures.md - comprehensive SOPs","+- [x] source/hankweave-docs/ - Nextra 4, Next.js 15, Bun install+build","+- [x] source/hankweave-docs/nextra-site/package.json - hankweave-docs v2.0.0","+- [x] source/hankweave-docs/nextra-site/vercel.json - framework: nextjs, bun install/build","+- [x] source/hankweave-docs/nextra-site/tsconfig.json - ES2020, strict:false, bundler resolution","+- [x] source/hankweave/tests/ - 65+ unit tests, 10+ integration, 10+ e2e, cross-runtime tests","+- [x] plan/background/background.md - pre-compiled research"," ","-## Areas to Read","+## Aha Moments"," ","-- [ ] source/hankweave/server - core runtime code","-- [ ] source/hankweave/package.json - dependencies, scripts","-- [ ] source/hankweave/scripts/ - build, release, sync, schema generation","-- [ ] source/hankweave/.github/workflows/ - CI/CD","-- [ ] source/hankweave/biome.json - linting config","-- [ ] source/hankweave/bunfig.toml - bun config","-- [ ] source/hankweave/tsconfig.json - typescript config","-- [ ] source/hankweave/standard-operating-procedures.md - conventions","-- [ ] source/hankweave-docs/ - docs site structure","-- [ ] source/hankweave/tests/ - test infrastructure","+### 1. The docs project has a nested structure","+The docs repo is `hankweave-docs/` at root but the actual Next.js project is at `hankweave-docs/nextra-site/`. The `package.json`, `tsconfig.json`, `next.config.mjs` etc. are all inside `nextra-site/`. The docs repo root has `README.md`, `.gitignore`, `notes/`, `plan/`."," ","-## Questions","+This means that wherever docs lands in the monorepo, you need to decide: does the workspace point to the `nextra-site/` subdirectory (where package.json lives), or to the outer `hankweave-docs/` directory?"," ","-1. What is the full directory structure of hankweave?","-2. How are scripts invoked (bun run, package.json scripts)?","-3. What does the sync-public script strip?","-4. What's the test infrastructure like?","-5. How deep is the server/ directory tree?","-6. What does the docs site structure look like?","+**Decision**: The workspace should point to where `package.json` is — so the `nextra-site/` level. The outer `hankweave-docs/` scaffold (README, notes, plan) is development overhead that probably doesn't need its own workspace and can either be flattened or kept as-is."," ","-## Aha Moments","+### 2. generate-schemas.ts is the only real cross-repo code reference","+Grep confirms only `scripts/generate-schemas.ts:28` references `hankweave-docs` in actual code. All other references are in `intermediates/` planning documents (which are stripped on release). This simplifies the migration — only one file needs a path adjustment."," ","-(to be filled as I read)","+### 3. The docs have NO dependency on the runtime package","+The docs site (`hankweave-docs`) doesn't `import` anything from `@southbridgeai/hankweave`. The only cross-repo data flow is schemas generated by the runtime and written to the docs `public/schemas/` directory. A `workspace:*` dependency is NOT needed at this time."," ","-## Tasks","+### 4. The public sync pipeline only needs to export the runtime","+The `sync-public.ts` script works from the root of the hankweave repo. In a monorepo, if the runtime stays at the root, the sync script barely changes. If it moves into a subdirectory, the strip lists and snapshot process need reworking."," ","-- Read through all source code systematically","-- Understand conventions and patterns","-- Draft plan","-- Review tests","-- Review docs","-- Write addendums","+### 5. CI uses composite actions with relative paths","+All three composite actions (`.github/actions/*`) are referenced as `./.github/actions/setup-environment` etc. These use relative paths. As long as the `.github/` directory stays at the monorepo root, they work. The composite actions themselves just do `bun install --frozen-lockfile` and `bun test` — they'd work with workspaces too.","+","+### 6. The release script assumes it runs from the repo root","+`release.ts` uses `resolve(process.cwd())` for paths and runs `git` commands. It also runs `bun run generate-schemas` and `bun run tc`. These all assume the cwd is the package root. In a monorepo with the runtime as a workspace, scripts would need to be run from the workspace directory or paths adjusted.","+","+### 7. build.ts uses relative paths from import.meta.dir","+`build.ts` resolves `distDir` as `join(import.meta.dir, \"..\", \"dist\")` and entry point as `\"./server/index.ts\"`. This means build.ts must run from (or be relative to) the runtime package root. Same for `build-executable.ts`.","+","+### 8. Vercel monorepo support just needs \"Root Directory\"","+Vercel lets you set the \"Root Directory\" for a project in project settings. The current `vercel.json` in `nextra-site/` would work if Vercel is pointed at the right subdirectory. This is a settings change, not a code change.","+","+### 9. The docs package.json has a `postbuild` step","+`\"postbuild\": \"pagefind --site .next/server/app --output-path public/_pagefind\"` — this runs Pagefind after `next build`. This would work fine in a workspace since `next build` runs from the workspace directory.","+","+### 10. tsconfig.json settings differ significantly between the two projects","+- Runtime: `target: ES2022`, `module: ES2022`, `moduleResolution: node`, `strict: true`, types: `bun-types`","+- Docs: `target: ES2020`, `module: esnext`, `moduleResolution: bundler`, `strict: false`, plugins: `next`","+","+These configs should NOT be shared. Each workspace needs its own `tsconfig.json`.","+","+### 11. There's only one bun.lock file per project","+Currently `source/hankweave/bun.lock` and `source/hankweave-docs/nextra-site/bun.lock`. In a monorepo, there would be a single root-level `bun.lock` file managed by Bun workspaces.","+","+### 12. The runtime's `.npmignore` strips `server/`, `scripts/`, `tests/` for npm publish","+npm publish only includes `dist/`, `shims/`, `schemas/`. This is controlled by both `.npmignore` and the `\"files\"` field in `package.json`. In a monorepo, the npm publish command would need to run from the runtime workspace. `\"files\"` field should continue to work correctly.","+","+## Key Decision Points","+","+### Structure Decision: Runtime at root vs in a subdirectory","+","+**Option A: Runtime stays at repo root, docs moves in as a workspace**","+```","+hankweave/                  (monorepo root = runtime package root)","+├── package.json            (root package.json with workspaces field, ALSO the runtime)","+├── server/","+├── scripts/","+├── tests/","+├── docs/                   (was: hankweave-docs/nextra-site)","+│   ├── package.json","+│   ├── app/","+│   ├── content/","+│   └── ...","+└── ...","+```","+- Pro: Minimal disruption to existing paths, scripts, CI","+- Pro: All existing relative paths in server/scripts still work","+- Con: The root package.json serves dual purpose (workspace root AND runtime package)","+- Con: `bun install` at root installs both workspace deps — might be slightly confusing","+","+**Option B: Both packages in subdirectories**","+```","+hankweave/                  (monorepo root)","+├── package.json            (workspace root, not a package itself)","+├── packages/","+│   ├── runtime/            (was: hankweave root)","+│   │   ├── package.json","+│   │   ├── server/","+│   │   └── ...","+│   └── docs/               (was: hankweave-docs/nextra-site)","+│       ├── package.json","+│       └── ...","+└── .github/","+```","+- Pro: Clean separation, conventional monorepo structure","+- Con: MASSIVE amount of path changes — every script, CI workflow, build config, `import.meta.dir` reference","+- Con: git history gets complicated","+- Con: The release.ts, sync-public.ts, build.ts all need significant reworking","+","+**Option C: Runtime at root, docs as apps/docs**","+```","+hankweave/                  (monorepo root = runtime package root)","+├── package.json            (root + runtime)","+├── server/","+├── apps/","+│   └── docs/               (was: hankweave-docs/nextra-site)","+│       ├── package.json","+│       └── ...","+└── ...","+```","+- Pro: Distinguishes \"apps\" (deployable) from packages (publishable) — common Turborepo pattern","+- Con: `apps/` is a Turborepo convention that might be overengineering for two packages","+- Otherwise similar to Option A","+","+### Recommendation: Option A (runtime at root)","+","+The path of least disruption. The runtime is the primary package — it has the most scripts, CI workflows, and tooling built around its position at root. Moving it into a subdirectory would require changing dozens of paths across scripts, workflows, and configs.","+","+The docs site is the smaller, simpler addition. Putting it at `docs/` (or `docs/nextra-site/`) within the runtime's repo structure is the minimal change.","+","+The trade-off: the root `package.json` serves double duty. But Bun workspaces handles this fine — you can have `\"workspaces\": [\"docs\"]` in the root and the root itself is also a package.","+","+### Sync-public impact","+","+With Option A (runtime at root), the sync-public.ts barely changes. The only new item is that the `docs/` directory needs to be added to the `STRIP_DIRS` list so it doesn't end up in the public runtime snapshot. Everything else stays the same.","+","+### CI impact","+","+With Option A:","+- `ci.yml` barely changes — the runtime is still at root, `bun install` installs all workspaces, tests run from root","+- `release.yml` same thing","+- Optional: Add path filtering so docs-only changes skip runtime tests (nice to have, not required initially)","+","+### generate-schemas.ts impact","+","+The `DOCS_SCHEMA_DIR` path changes from `../../hankweave-docs/nextra-site/public/schemas` to `../docs/public/schemas` (one level up from `scripts/`, then into `docs/`). This is actually simpler.","+","+## Questions Resolved","+","+1. **Full directory structure** — mapped exhaustively","+2. **Script invocation** — all via `bun run <script>` or `bun scripts/<file>.ts`","+3. **Sync-public strips** — STRIP_DIRS + STRIP_FILES documented, understood","+4. **Test infrastructure** — bun:test, 65+ unit, 10+ integration, e2e with mock LLMs","+5. **server/ depth** — 3-4 levels (server/sentinels/, server/llm/, server/types/, etc.)","+6. **Docs structure** — Nextra 4 + Next.js 15, content/ for MDX, app/ for routes, components/ for custom","+","+## Questions Remaining","+","+1. **Should the outer hankweave-docs/ wrapper be kept?** — The docs repo has a root-level README, notes/, plan/ directory outside nextra-site/. These are dev-only files. Probably flatten: just bring nextra-site/ contents into docs/.","+2. **Shared dev dependencies?** — Both projects use TypeScript. The docs use `@types/node` and `@types/react`. The runtime uses `@types/bun`. Bun catalogs could share TypeScript version, but it's not critical.","+3. **Does the monorepo need its own README at root?** — The current runtime README is internal. The public one is in `public-release-files/`. Adding a monorepo root README could be helpful but isn't strictly necessary for the migration."]}],"originalFile":"# Planning Log: Hankweave Monorepo Migration\n\n## Initial Observations from Task\n\n- Task is to migrate from multi-repo to monorepo (hankweave runtime + hankweave-docs)\n- There's already a cross-repo dependency via `generate-schemas.ts` that hardcodes sibling paths\n- Three repos involved: private dev (hankweave), docs (hankweave-docs), public mirror (hankweave-runtime)\n- Key open questions: repo structure, public release pipeline, CI changes, Vercel config\n- Bun workspaces is the natural fit since project already uses Bun\n\n## Areas to Read\n\n- [ ] source/hankweave/server - core runtime code\n- [ ] source/hankweave/package.json - dependencies, scripts\n- [ ] source/hankweave/scripts/ - build, release, sync, schema generation\n- [ ] source/hankweave/.github/workflows/ - CI/CD\n- [ ] source/hankweave/biome.json - linting config\n- [ ] source/hankweave/bunfig.toml - bun config\n- [ ] source/hankweave/tsconfig.json - typescript config\n- [ ] source/hankweave/standard-operating-procedures.md - conventions\n- [ ] source/hankweave-docs/ - docs site structure\n- [ ] source/hankweave/tests/ - test infrastructure\n\n## Questions\n\n1. What is the full directory structure of hankweave?\n2. How are scripts invoked (bun run, package.json scripts)?\n3. What does the sync-public script strip?\n4. What's the test infrastructure like?\n5. How deep is the server/ directory tree?\n6. What does the docs site structure look like?\n\n## Aha Moments\n\n(to be filled as I read)\n\n## Tasks\n\n- Read through all source code systematically\n- Understand conventions and patterns\n- Draft plan\n- Review tests\n- Review docs\n- Write addendums\n"},"timestamp":"2026-03-05T12:15:22.722Z"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01KgD9TcvYvvus2mzc5U4NgT","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Now let me write the main plan."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":6890,"cache_read_input_tokens":127587,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":6890},"output_tokens":1,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"6f6abf3a-0b5f-4251-a578-7f2d66ac9b9a","uuid":"b70f8bec-94f3-4aca-8a21-58a635bde443","timestamp":"2026-03-05T12:15:27.323Z"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01KgD9TcvYvvus2mzc5U4NgT","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_016oeLnwHKKBxKGWySvrwQpo","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/Users/philip/projects/hw-budget-tests/plan-gen-execution/agentRoot/plan/rough-workspace/opus/plan.md","content":"# Plan: Migrate Hankweave to Monorepo\n\n## a. The Main Task\n\nConsolidate the `SouthBridgeAI/hankweave` (runtime) and `SouthBridgeAI/hankweave-docs` (documentation site) repositories into a single monorepo using Bun workspaces. The runtime package stays at the repository root; the docs site moves in as a workspace at `docs/`. The existing CI/CD pipelines, release process, and public sync workflow continue to function with minimal changes.\n\n---\n\n## b. Information Available\n\n### Sources that informed this plan\n\n1. **Task specification** — `plan/task.md` (the task description, open questions, and research)\n2. **Background research** — `plan/background/background.md` (Bun workspace docs, Turborepo evaluation, git subtree options)\n3. **Full source code** — Read every config, script, workflow, and representative server/test files from both repos\n4. **SOP document** — `source/hankweave/standard-operating-procedures.md` (branch model, release process, AI agent policy)\n\n### What I could see\n\n- Full directory structure and file contents of both `source/hankweave/` and `source/hankweave-docs/`\n- All CI workflows, composite actions, and build/release/sync scripts\n- Test infrastructure (patterns, paths, utilities, config fixtures)\n- Both `package.json` files, `tsconfig.json` files, `biome.json`, `bunfig.toml`\n- The docs Nextra/Next.js setup including `vercel.json`, `next.config.mjs`, `app/`, `content/`\n\n### What I could NOT see\n\n- The actual `SouthBridgeAI/hankweave-runtime` (public mirror) repo — only references to it in scripts and workflows\n- Vercel project settings (the current \"Root Directory\" config for docs deployment)\n- GitHub repo settings (branch protection rules, secrets configuration)\n- Linear project (no ticket ID was provided)\n- Whether there are any other repositories or tools the task spec's \"related packages/tools\" refers to beyond what's in the source snapshot\n\n### Assumptions made\n\n1. The `hankweave-docs` repo has no significant git history worth preserving (the task says \"nice to have\" for history)\n2. The public mirror (`hankweave-runtime`) continues to exist as a separate repo — this migration consolidates the two **private development** repos\n3. The docs site continues to deploy to Vercel from the same repo (now a monorepo)\n4. No Turborepo or other task orchestration is needed for two packages\n\n---\n\n## c. Sources and Related Things\n\n### Original tickets/tasks\n\n- Task description: `plan/task.md`\n- No Linear ticket ID provided\n\n### Code references\n\n| File | Line(s) | Relevance |\n|------|---------|-----------|\n| `source/hankweave/package.json` | Full | Root package.json — needs `workspaces` field added |\n| `source/hankweave-docs/nextra-site/package.json` | Full | Docs package.json — moves to `docs/package.json` |\n| `source/hankweave/scripts/generate-schemas.ts` | 24-32 | The only cross-repo code dependency; path to docs schemas dir |\n| `source/hankweave/scripts/sync-public.ts` | 28-44 | STRIP_DIRS/STRIP_FILES — needs `docs` added to strip list |\n| `source/hankweave/scripts/release.ts` | 54-60 | INTERNAL_FILES_TO_STRIP — needs `docs/` added |\n| `source/hankweave/.github/workflows/ci.yml` | Full | CI workflow — minimal changes needed |\n| `source/hankweave/.github/workflows/release.yml` | Full | Release workflow — minimal changes |\n| `source/hankweave/.github/workflows/sync-public.yml` | Full | Sync workflow — minimal changes |\n| `source/hankweave/.github/actions/setup-environment/action.yml` | Full | Composite action — `bun install` already handles workspaces |\n| `source/hankweave/biome.json` | Full | Linting scope — currently only covers runtime files |\n| `source/hankweave/bunfig.toml` | Full | Bun config — no changes needed |\n| `source/hankweave/tsconfig.json` | Full | Runtime TypeScript config — stays as-is |\n| `source/hankweave-docs/nextra-site/tsconfig.json` | Full | Docs TypeScript config — stays as-is in new location |\n| `source/hankweave-docs/nextra-site/vercel.json` | Full | Vercel config — stays in docs workspace |\n| `source/hankweave-docs/nextra-site/next.config.mjs` | Full | Next.js config — no changes |\n| `source/hankweave/standard-operating-procedures.md` | 10-30, 34-36 | Repository structure section — needs update |\n| `source/hankweave/.gitignore` | Full | May need additions for docs build artifacts |\n| `source/hankweave/scripts/test-sync-local.ts` | 159-175 | Verification list — needs `docs` added |\n\n### Research sources\n\n- [Bun Workspaces](https://bun.sh/docs/install/workspaces) — native workspace support via `package.json` `workspaces` field\n- [Bun Catalogs](https://bun.sh/docs/install/catalogs) — optional shared dependency versioning (not needed initially)\n- [git subtree merges](https://docs.github.com/en/get-started/using-git/about-git-subtree-merges) — for preserving docs repo history\n\n---\n\n## d. Conventions and Patterns\n\n### Observed code conventions\n\n1. **Scripts are TypeScript** — all build/release/sync scripts are `.ts` files run with `bun`\n2. **Relative paths from `import.meta.dir`** — scripts use `path.join(import.meta.dir, \"..\")` to resolve project root\n3. **`Bun.spawn` for shell commands** — scripts use `Bun.spawn([\"sh\", \"-c\", cmd])` rather than Node's `child_process`\n4. **Two-space indent, 100-char line width** — enforced by Biome\n5. **LF line endings** — enforced by `.gitattributes`\n6. **No shared state between packages** — docs don't import from runtime; only schema files are copied\n7. **Strip lists are explicit** — both `sync-public.ts` and `release.ts` maintain explicit lists of files/dirs to remove\n\n### Patterns to follow\n\n- Add `docs` to existing strip lists rather than creating new mechanisms\n- Keep the root `package.json` as the runtime's package.json (add `workspaces` field only)\n- Don't introduce Turborepo or other tooling — Bun workspaces is sufficient\n- Keep separate `tsconfig.json` per workspace (they have incompatible settings)\n- Don't introduce workspace dependencies (`workspace:*`) between docs and runtime — the schema copy is a script-level operation\n\n### Patterns to avoid\n\n- Don't move the runtime into a subdirectory (would break dozens of paths)\n- Don't create a shared `tsconfig.base.json` (the two configs are too different)\n- Don't add a `bun` workspace filter to CI scripts (just let everything install and run from root)\n\n---\n\n## e. Decision Points and Judgement Calls\n\n### Decision 1: Keep the runtime at the repo root\n\n**Chosen**: The runtime stays at root. The docs site moves into `docs/`.\n\n**Reasoning**: The runtime has 6 scripts, 3 CI workflows, 3 composite actions, build/release/sync pipelines, and extensive relative path usage. Moving it into a subdirectory would require changing every one of these. The docs site has no CI of its own (Vercel builds it), no complex scripts, and one `package.json`. Moving the docs in is a fraction of the work.\n\n**Trade-off**: The root `package.json` serves dual purpose as both workspace root and runtime package. This is a recognized Bun workspace pattern and works correctly — the root package is an \"implicit workspace member\" by default.\n\n**Impact**: Minimal disruption to existing workflows. The main changes are:\n- Adding `\"workspaces\": [\"docs\"]` to root `package.json`\n- Updating 2-3 file paths in scripts\n- Adding `docs` to strip lists\n\n### Decision 2: Flatten the docs repo structure\n\n**Chosen**: Move `hankweave-docs/nextra-site/*` contents directly into `docs/` (not `docs/nextra-site/`).\n\n**Reasoning**: The `nextra-site/` wrapper exists because the docs repo has a root with README, notes, and plan directories. In the monorepo, these don't need a separate layer. The `package.json`, `tsconfig.json`, `next.config.mjs`, and all source files go directly in `docs/`.\n\n**Alternative considered**: Keeping `docs/nextra-site/` as-is. This preserves the original structure but adds an unnecessary level of nesting. It also means `\"workspaces\": [\"docs/nextra-site\"]` which is less clean.\n\n**What to do with `hankweave-docs/` non-nextra files**: The `README.md` at docs repo root, `notes/`, and `plan/` directories are development artifacts. They can either:\n- Be discarded (they're internal planning docs)\n- Be moved to an `intermediates/` entry documenting the docs site\n- Be kept at `docs/` level alongside the nextra files\n\n**Recommendation**: Discard `notes/` and `plan/` (they're historical planning docs). Keep the `README.md` as `docs/README.md` if desired, but it's not required.\n\n### Decision 3: Don't use `workspace:*` dependencies\n\n**Chosen**: No workspace dependency between docs and runtime.\n\n**Reasoning**: The docs site doesn't import from the runtime. The only data flow is schema JSON files copied by `generate-schemas.ts`. This is a script-level file copy, not a package dependency. Adding a `workspace:*` dependency would incorrectly suggest the docs depend on the runtime as a package.\n\n**When to revisit**: If the docs site ever needs to import TypeScript types or utilities from the runtime (e.g., for auto-generated API reference), add `workspace:*` at that point.\n\n### Decision 4: Convert the existing hankweave repo, don't create new\n\n**Chosen**: Modify `SouthBridgeAI/hankweave` in-place to become the monorepo.\n\n**Reasoning**: All CI workflows, GitHub secrets, branch protection rules, Linear integration, and team permissions are already configured on this repo. Creating a new repo would require reconfiguring everything. The existing git history stays intact. The docs files are brought in via a merge commit (or `git subtree add` if history preservation is desired).\n\n**Impact**: No disruption to existing CI/CD. No need to update secrets, protections, or integrations.\n\n### Decision 5: No Turborepo\n\n**Chosen**: Use Bun workspaces alone, no Turborepo.\n\n**Reasoning**: Two packages don't need task orchestration or caching. The runtime builds and tests independently. The docs site builds independently on Vercel. There's no dependency chain requiring `^build` semantics. Adding Turborepo would increase complexity with no clear benefit.\n\n**When to revisit**: If more packages are added (e.g., a shared types package, a CLI package), or if CI times become a problem and caching would help.\n\n### Decision 6: Single lockfile at root\n\n**Chosen**: One `bun.lock` at the repo root, replacing both existing lockfiles.\n\n**Reasoning**: Bun workspaces uses a single root lockfile. Both workspace packages' dependencies are resolved and locked together. This is how Bun workspaces work by default — there's no option for per-workspace lockfiles.\n\n**Impact**: The `bun.lock` from `hankweave` and the `bun.lock` from `hankweave-docs/nextra-site` are both replaced by a regenerated root `bun.lock`. The first `bun install` after migration will generate this.\n\n### Over/under engineering line\n\n- **Under**: Not adding path filtering to CI (docs changes trigger runtime tests). Acceptable for now — CI runs are fast and infrequent enough.\n- **Under**: Not using Bun catalogs for shared TypeScript version. Acceptable — the projects only share TypeScript and it's fine to have independent versions.\n- **Over**: Adding Turborepo, shared configs, workspace link scripts, or monorepo-specific CI tooling. Not needed for two packages.\n- **Right level**: Adding `docs` to strip lists, updating one path in `generate-schemas.ts`, updating the SOP doc. Minimal changes that make the monorepo work.\n\n---\n\n## f. The Actual Implementation Plan\n\n### Phase 0: Preparation\n\n**Step 0.1: Create a feature branch**\n```bash\ngit checkout develop\ngit pull origin develop\ngit checkout -b hrishi/monorepo-migration  # or similar\n```\n\n**Step 0.2: Verify clean state**\n- Ensure all tests pass: `bun run test:unit`, `bun run lint`, `bun run tc`\n- Ensure working directory is clean: `git status`\n\n### Phase 1: Bring docs into the repo\n\n**Step 1.1: Create the `docs/` directory**\n\nCopy the contents of `hankweave-docs/nextra-site/` into `docs/` at the runtime repo root.\n\nFiles to copy from `hankweave-docs/nextra-site/`:\n```\napp/\ncomponents/\ncontent/\npublic/\nstyles/\nbun.lock           (will be removed later — root lockfile takes over)\nmdx-components.tsx\nnext-env.d.ts\nnext.config.mjs\npackage.json\ntsconfig.json\nvercel.json\n```\n\nFiles from `hankweave-docs/` root to optionally include:\n```\n.gitignore          → merge relevant rules into root .gitignore\nREADME.md           → can include as docs/README.md (internal docs readme)\n```\n\nFiles to NOT copy:\n```\nnotes/              → historical planning docs, discard\nplan/               → historical planning docs, discard\n```\n\n**Step 1.2: Add workspace configuration to root `package.json`**\n\nModify `source/hankweave/package.json` — add `workspaces` field:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"name\": \"@southbridgeai/hankweave\",\n  \"version\": \"0.5.6\",\n  \"workspaces\": [\"docs\"],\n  ...\n}\n```\n\n**Step 1.3: Delete the docs-side `bun.lock`**\n\nRemove `docs/bun.lock` (the old `nextra-site/bun.lock`). The root lockfile will manage all dependencies.\n\n**Step 1.4: Run `bun install`**\n\nFrom the repo root, run `bun install`. This:\n- Resolves dependencies for both workspaces\n- Generates a new unified `bun.lock` at root\n- Creates a single `node_modules/` tree\n\nVerify: `docs/node_modules` should be a symlink or the workspace should be hoisted.\n\n**Step 1.5: Verify docs build locally**\n\n```bash\ncd docs && bun run build\n```\n\nVerify the Next.js build succeeds. If there are issues with module resolution, they'll surface here.\n\n### Phase 2: Update cross-repo references\n\n**Step 2.1: Update `scripts/generate-schemas.ts`**\n\nChange the `DOCS_SCHEMA_DIR` path from:\n```typescript\nconst DOCS_SCHEMA_DIR = path.join(\n  import.meta.dir,\n  \"..\",\n  \"..\",\n  \"hankweave-docs\",\n  \"nextra-site\",\n  \"public\",\n  \"schemas\",\n);\n```\n\nTo:\n```typescript\nconst DOCS_SCHEMA_DIR = path.join(\n  import.meta.dir,\n  \"..\",\n  \"docs\",\n  \"public\",\n  \"schemas\",\n);\n```\n\nThis is the only code change needed to formalize the cross-package dependency. The `docsAvailable` check at line ~77 will now reliably find the docs directory (it's always present in the monorepo, not dependent on sibling checkout).\n\n**Step 2.2: Run `bun run generate-schemas` to verify**\n\nVerify schemas are written to both `schemas/` and `docs/public/schemas/`.\n\n### Phase 3: Update strip lists for public sync\n\n**Step 3.1: Update `scripts/sync-public.ts` STRIP_DIRS**\n\nAt line 28, add `\"docs\"` to the STRIP_DIRS array:\n```typescript\nconst STRIP_DIRS = [\"intermediates\", \"external-docs\", \"public-release-files\", \".github/actions\", \"docs\"];\n```\n\nThis ensures the docs workspace doesn't end up in the public runtime snapshot.\n\n**Step 3.2: Update `scripts/release.ts` INTERNAL_FILES_TO_STRIP**\n\nAt line 54, add `\"docs/\"` to the strip list:\n```typescript\nconst INTERNAL_FILES_TO_STRIP = [\n  \"intermediates/\",\n  \"external-docs/\",\n  \"CLAUDE.md\",\n  \"CONTRIBUTING.md\",\n  \"standard-operating-procedures.md\",\n  \"docs/\",\n];\n```\n\nThis ensures the docs workspace is stripped from `release/alpha` during the release process.\n\n**Step 3.3: Update `scripts/test-sync-local.ts` verification list**\n\nAt line 160, add `\"docs\"` to the `shouldNotExist` array:\n```typescript\nconst shouldNotExist = [\n  \"intermediates\",\n  \"external-docs\",\n  // ... existing entries ...\n  \"docs\",  // Docs workspace should not be in public repo\n];\n```\n\n### Phase 4: Update .gitignore\n\n**Step 4.1: Merge docs-specific ignores into root `.gitignore`**\n\nAdd the following to the root `.gitignore` (from `hankweave-docs/.gitignore`):\n\n```gitignore\n# Docs build output\ndocs/.next/\ndocs/out/\n\n# Pagefind search index (generated at build time)\ndocs/public/_pagefind/\n```\n\nMost entries from the docs `.gitignore` (like `node_modules/`, `.env`, `.DS_Store`) are already covered by the root `.gitignore`.\n\n### Phase 5: Update documentation\n\n**Step 5.1: Update `standard-operating-procedures.md` repository structure**\n\nUpdate the repo structure section (lines 10-30) to reflect the monorepo:\n\n```\nhankweave-dev/              ← private repo (SouthBridgeAI/hankweave)\n├── server/                 ← runtime source code (TypeScript, Bun)\n├── scripts/                ← build, release, sync, and dev tooling\n├── tests/                  ← unit, integration, e2e, cross-runtime\n├── schemas/                ← generated JSON schemas (from Zod)\n├── shims/                  ← agent harness adapters\n├── docs/                   ← documentation site (Next.js + Nextra, Bun workspace)\n│   ├── app/                ← Next.js app directory\n│   ├── content/            ← MDX documentation pages\n│   ├── components/         ← custom React components\n│   ├── public/             ← static assets, schemas, images\n│   └── package.json        ← hankweave-docs workspace\n├── learning/               ← example hanks and guides (ships to public)\n├── intermediates/          ← planning docs, numbered by feature (internal only)\n├── external-docs/          ← SDK reference docs for agent context (internal only)\n├── public-release-files/   ← files overlaid onto the public repo during sync\n├── assets/                 ← images, demo GIFs\n├── .github/                ← CI workflows and composite actions\n├── CHANGELOG.md            ← Keep a Changelog format, [Unreleased] workflow\n├── CONTRIBUTING.md         ← branch model and PR guidelines (internal only)\n└── package.json            ← @southbridgeai/hankweave (private npm) + workspace root\n```\n\n**Step 5.2: Update the \"What gets stripped\" section**\n\nAdd `docs/` to the list of stripped directories.\n\n**Step 5.3: Update the schema generation note**\n\nIf there's any documentation about the cross-repo schema dependency, update it to note that schemas are now generated directly into `docs/public/schemas/` (same repo).\n\n### Phase 6: Verify everything works\n\n**Step 6.1: Run the full test suite**\n```bash\nbun run lint\nbun run tc\nbun run test:unit\nbun run test:integration\n```\n\n**Step 6.2: Run schema generation**\n```bash\nbun run generate-schemas\n```\nVerify output in both `schemas/` and `docs/public/schemas/`.\n\n**Step 6.3: Run the build**\n```bash\nbun run build\n```\nVerify `dist/index.js` is created.\n\n**Step 6.4: Verify docs build**\n```bash\ncd docs && bun run build\n```\n\n**Step 6.5: Test the release dry-run**\n```bash\nbun scripts/release.ts patch --dry-run --skip-checks\n```\nVerify validation passes (if on develop with clean tree).\n\n**Step 6.6: Test the sync dry-run**\n```bash\nbun scripts/test-sync-local.ts --version <current-version> --use-head\n```\nVerify `docs/` is correctly stripped from the snapshot and the verification checks pass.\n\n### Phase 7: Vercel configuration (out-of-band)\n\n**Step 7.1: Update Vercel project settings**\n\nThis is a manual change in the Vercel dashboard, not a code change:\n\n1. Go to the Vercel project for `hankweave-docs`\n2. Go to Settings → General → Root Directory\n3. Change from the current setting (either `/` or `nextra-site/`) to `docs/`\n4. Verify the Framework Preset is still \"Next.js\"\n5. The `vercel.json` inside `docs/` will continue to work\n\n**Step 7.2: Connect Vercel to the monorepo**\n\nIf the Vercel project is currently connected to `SouthBridgeAI/hankweave-docs`:\n1. Disconnect from `hankweave-docs`\n2. Connect to `SouthBridgeAI/hankweave`\n3. Set Root Directory to `docs/`\n4. Vercel will deploy when changes are pushed to the configured branch\n\nNote: Vercel can be configured to only deploy when files in the `docs/` directory change, using the \"Ignored Build Step\" setting: `git diff --quiet HEAD^ HEAD -- docs/`.\n\n### Phase 8: Commit and PR\n\n**Step 8.1: Commit the migration**\n\nCommit all changes as a single atomic commit on the feature branch:\n```\nMigrate to monorepo: bring docs into workspace\n\n- Add docs/ workspace (formerly SouthBridgeAI/hankweave-docs)\n- Add \"workspaces\" field to root package.json\n- Update generate-schemas.ts to use monorepo-local docs path\n- Add docs/ to strip lists (sync-public.ts, release.ts, test-sync-local.ts)\n- Update .gitignore for docs build artifacts\n- Update standard-operating-procedures.md\n```\n\n**Step 8.2: Open PR targeting develop**\n\nFollow existing PR conventions:\n- Title: `Monorepo migration: consolidate hankweave-docs into workspace`\n- Body: Problem/approach/files format per SOP section 4\n\n---\n\n## g. Testing Considerations\n\n### Existing tests affected\n\n**None of the existing unit, integration, or E2E tests should be affected.** The runtime code is unchanged — no server files, no type files, no config files are modified. Only scripts and metadata files are touched.\n\nThe tests that could theoretically be affected:\n\n1. **`tests/unit/json-schema.test.ts`** — Tests that schemas validate correctly. These read from `schemas/` directory which is unchanged.\n2. **E2E init tests** — Test the `--init` scaffolding flow. The runtime code is unchanged so these should pass.\n3. **Any test that reads `package.json`** — The only change is adding a `\"workspaces\"` field, which shouldn't affect anything that reads version, name, or dependencies.\n\n### New tests needed\n\nNo new automated tests are strictly required. The migration is a structural change, not a code change. Verification is manual:\n\n1. `bun install` succeeds at root and installs both workspaces\n2. `bun run build` still produces correct dist output\n3. `bun run generate-schemas` writes to both locations\n4. `docs/` build works: `cd docs && bun run build`\n5. Sync dry-run strips `docs/` correctly\n\n### Edge cases to consider\n\n1. **npm publish from root** — `npm publish` uses `\"files\"` field. The `docs/` directory is NOT in the `\"files\"` array (`[\"dist\", \"shims\", \"schemas\"]`), so it won't be included in the npm package. Verify this with `npm pack --dry-run`.\n2. **CI `bun install --frozen-lockfile`** — The new unified `bun.lock` needs to be committed. If it's not, CI will fail because `--frozen-lockfile` doesn't allow lockfile generation.\n3. **`.npmignore` and docs** — The `.npmignore` doesn't need `docs/` added because the `\"files\"` field in `package.json` is an allowlist — only listed paths are included.\n\n---\n\n## h. Documentation Considerations\n\n### Docs to update\n\n1. **`standard-operating-procedures.md`** — Repository structure diagram, strip list documentation (see Phase 5)\n2. **This plan itself** — Should be filed as an intermediate (e.g., `intermediates/45-monorepo-migration/`)\n\n### New docs needed\n\nNone required. The monorepo migration is an internal infrastructure change. The docs site content (`docs/content/`) doesn't need updating — it documents Hankweave the product, not the development repo structure.\n\n### Docs that should NOT be updated\n\n- The user-facing docs (`docs/content/guides/getting-started.mdx`, etc.) — these reference `hankweave` as a CLI tool, not as a repo structure\n- The public `README.md` (in `public-release-files/`) — this describes the public `hankweave-runtime` repo, not the private dev repo\n\n---\n\n## i. Additional Resources\n\n### Bun workspaces\n\n- [bun.sh/docs/install/workspaces](https://bun.sh/docs/install/workspaces) — primary reference\n- Root package can be a workspace member itself (important for our \"runtime at root\" approach)\n- `bun install` at root installs all workspace dependencies\n- `bun --filter <name> <script>` runs scripts in specific workspaces (useful for `bun --filter hankweave-docs build`)\n\n### Vercel monorepo support\n\n- Vercel's Root Directory setting specifies which subdirectory contains the deployable project\n- \"Ignored Build Step\" can filter deploys to only docs changes\n\n### Git subtree (optional history preservation)\n\nIf docs history is desired:\n```bash\ngit remote add -f hankweave-docs <url>\ngit merge -s ours --no-commit --allow-unrelated-histories hankweave-docs/main\ngit read-tree --prefix=docs/ -u hankweave-docs/main\ngit commit -m \"Merge hankweave-docs into monorepo as docs workspace\"\n```\n\nThis preserves the full commit history of `hankweave-docs` at the `docs/` prefix. After this, remove the files from `hankweave-docs/` root that aren't needed (README, notes/, plan/) and restructure so the nextra-site contents are at `docs/` root.\n\nAlternatively, a simple file copy with a \"bring docs into monorepo\" commit is simpler and the docs repo's history is available in the original repo if ever needed.\n\n---\n\n## Summary of Changes\n\n| File | Change Type | Description |\n|------|-------------|-------------|\n| `package.json` | Modify | Add `\"workspaces\": [\"docs\"]` |\n| `docs/` | Create (directory) | Contains contents of `hankweave-docs/nextra-site/` |\n| `scripts/generate-schemas.ts` | Modify | Update `DOCS_SCHEMA_DIR` path (line 24-32) |\n| `scripts/sync-public.ts` | Modify | Add `\"docs\"` to `STRIP_DIRS` (line 28) |\n| `scripts/release.ts` | Modify | Add `\"docs/\"` to `INTERNAL_FILES_TO_STRIP` (line 54) |\n| `scripts/test-sync-local.ts` | Modify | Add `\"docs\"` to `shouldNotExist` (line 160) |\n| `.gitignore` | Modify | Add docs build artifacts |\n| `standard-operating-procedures.md` | Modify | Update repo structure diagram |\n| `bun.lock` | Regenerate | New unified lockfile |\n\n**Total files modified**: 7 existing files + 1 new directory of ~30 files\n**Total lines of code changed**: ~20 lines across scripts + SOP update\n\nThis is deliberately minimal. The migration's success is measured by how little changes while still achieving the goal: a reliable, explicit relationship between runtime and docs in a single repo.\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":6890,"cache_read_input_tokens":127587,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":6890},"output_tokens":7366,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"6f6abf3a-0b5f-4251-a578-7f2d66ac9b9a","uuid":"4173545b-a702-43ac-ad8b-9d9a8e6669b9","timestamp":"2026-03-05T12:17:56.010Z"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_016oeLnwHKKBxKGWySvrwQpo","type":"tool_result","content":"File created successfully at: /Users/philip/projects/hw-budget-tests/plan-gen-execution/agentRoot/plan/rough-workspace/opus/plan.md"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"6f6abf3a-0b5f-4251-a578-7f2d66ac9b9a","uuid":"729f8d4b-5c2d-4c14-adaa-6acc911ee7e3","tool_use_result":{"type":"create","filePath":"/Users/philip/projects/hw-budget-tests/plan-gen-execution/agentRoot/plan/rough-workspace/opus/plan.md","content":"# Plan: Migrate Hankweave to Monorepo\n\n## a. The Main Task\n\nConsolidate the `SouthBridgeAI/hankweave` (runtime) and `SouthBridgeAI/hankweave-docs` (documentation site) repositories into a single monorepo using Bun workspaces. The runtime package stays at the repository root; the docs site moves in as a workspace at `docs/`. The existing CI/CD pipelines, release process, and public sync workflow continue to function with minimal changes.\n\n---\n\n## b. Information Available\n\n### Sources that informed this plan\n\n1. **Task specification** — `plan/task.md` (the task description, open questions, and research)\n2. **Background research** — `plan/background/background.md` (Bun workspace docs, Turborepo evaluation, git subtree options)\n3. **Full source code** — Read every config, script, workflow, and representative server/test files from both repos\n4. **SOP document** — `source/hankweave/standard-operating-procedures.md` (branch model, release process, AI agent policy)\n\n### What I could see\n\n- Full directory structure and file contents of both `source/hankweave/` and `source/hankweave-docs/`\n- All CI workflows, composite actions, and build/release/sync scripts\n- Test infrastructure (patterns, paths, utilities, config fixtures)\n- Both `package.json` files, `tsconfig.json` files, `biome.json`, `bunfig.toml`\n- The docs Nextra/Next.js setup including `vercel.json`, `next.config.mjs`, `app/`, `content/`\n\n### What I could NOT see\n\n- The actual `SouthBridgeAI/hankweave-runtime` (public mirror) repo — only references to it in scripts and workflows\n- Vercel project settings (the current \"Root Directory\" config for docs deployment)\n- GitHub repo settings (branch protection rules, secrets configuration)\n- Linear project (no ticket ID was provided)\n- Whether there are any other repositories or tools the task spec's \"related packages/tools\" refers to beyond what's in the source snapshot\n\n### Assumptions made\n\n1. The `hankweave-docs` repo has no significant git history worth preserving (the task says \"nice to have\" for history)\n2. The public mirror (`hankweave-runtime`) continues to exist as a separate repo — this migration consolidates the two **private development** repos\n3. The docs site continues to deploy to Vercel from the same repo (now a monorepo)\n4. No Turborepo or other task orchestration is needed for two packages\n\n---\n\n## c. Sources and Related Things\n\n### Original tickets/tasks\n\n- Task description: `plan/task.md`\n- No Linear ticket ID provided\n\n### Code references\n\n| File | Line(s) | Relevance |\n|------|---------|-----------|\n| `source/hankweave/package.json` | Full | Root package.json — needs `workspaces` field added |\n| `source/hankweave-docs/nextra-site/package.json` | Full | Docs package.json — moves to `docs/package.json` |\n| `source/hankweave/scripts/generate-schemas.ts` | 24-32 | The only cross-repo code dependency; path to docs schemas dir |\n| `source/hankweave/scripts/sync-public.ts` | 28-44 | STRIP_DIRS/STRIP_FILES — needs `docs` added to strip list |\n| `source/hankweave/scripts/release.ts` | 54-60 | INTERNAL_FILES_TO_STRIP — needs `docs/` added |\n| `source/hankweave/.github/workflows/ci.yml` | Full | CI workflow — minimal changes needed |\n| `source/hankweave/.github/workflows/release.yml` | Full | Release workflow — minimal changes |\n| `source/hankweave/.github/workflows/sync-public.yml` | Full | Sync workflow — minimal changes |\n| `source/hankweave/.github/actions/setup-environment/action.yml` | Full | Composite action — `bun install` already handles workspaces |\n| `source/hankweave/biome.json` | Full | Linting scope — currently only covers runtime files |\n| `source/hankweave/bunfig.toml` | Full | Bun config — no changes needed |\n| `source/hankweave/tsconfig.json` | Full | Runtime TypeScript config — stays as-is |\n| `source/hankweave-docs/nextra-site/tsconfig.json` | Full | Docs TypeScript config — stays as-is in new location |\n| `source/hankweave-docs/nextra-site/vercel.json` | Full | Vercel config — stays in docs workspace |\n| `source/hankweave-docs/nextra-site/next.config.mjs` | Full | Next.js config — no changes |\n| `source/hankweave/standard-operating-procedures.md` | 10-30, 34-36 | Repository structure section — needs update |\n| `source/hankweave/.gitignore` | Full | May need additions for docs build artifacts |\n| `source/hankweave/scripts/test-sync-local.ts` | 159-175 | Verification list — needs `docs` added |\n\n### Research sources\n\n- [Bun Workspaces](https://bun.sh/docs/install/workspaces) — native workspace support via `package.json` `workspaces` field\n- [Bun Catalogs](https://bun.sh/docs/install/catalogs) — optional shared dependency versioning (not needed initially)\n- [git subtree merges](https://docs.github.com/en/get-started/using-git/about-git-subtree-merges) — for preserving docs repo history\n\n---\n\n## d. Conventions and Patterns\n\n### Observed code conventions\n\n1. **Scripts are TypeScript** — all build/release/sync scripts are `.ts` files run with `bun`\n2. **Relative paths from `import.meta.dir`** — scripts use `path.join(import.meta.dir, \"..\")` to resolve project root\n3. **`Bun.spawn` for shell commands** — scripts use `Bun.spawn([\"sh\", \"-c\", cmd])` rather than Node's `child_process`\n4. **Two-space indent, 100-char line width** — enforced by Biome\n5. **LF line endings** — enforced by `.gitattributes`\n6. **No shared state between packages** — docs don't import from runtime; only schema files are copied\n7. **Strip lists are explicit** — both `sync-public.ts` and `release.ts` maintain explicit lists of files/dirs to remove\n\n### Patterns to follow\n\n- Add `docs` to existing strip lists rather than creating new mechanisms\n- Keep the root `package.json` as the runtime's package.json (add `workspaces` field only)\n- Don't introduce Turborepo or other tooling — Bun workspaces is sufficient\n- Keep separate `tsconfig.json` per workspace (they have incompatible settings)\n- Don't introduce workspace dependencies (`workspace:*`) between docs and runtime — the schema copy is a script-level operation\n\n### Patterns to avoid\n\n- Don't move the runtime into a subdirectory (would break dozens of paths)\n- Don't create a shared `tsconfig.base.json` (the two configs are too different)\n- Don't add a `bun` workspace filter to CI scripts (just let everything install and run from root)\n\n---\n\n## e. Decision Points and Judgement Calls\n\n### Decision 1: Keep the runtime at the repo root\n\n**Chosen**: The runtime stays at root. The docs site moves into `docs/`.\n\n**Reasoning**: The runtime has 6 scripts, 3 CI workflows, 3 composite actions, build/release/sync pipelines, and extensive relative path usage. Moving it into a subdirectory would require changing every one of these. The docs site has no CI of its own (Vercel builds it), no complex scripts, and one `package.json`. Moving the docs in is a fraction of the work.\n\n**Trade-off**: The root `package.json` serves dual purpose as both workspace root and runtime package. This is a recognized Bun workspace pattern and works correctly — the root package is an \"implicit workspace member\" by default.\n\n**Impact**: Minimal disruption to existing workflows. The main changes are:\n- Adding `\"workspaces\": [\"docs\"]` to root `package.json`\n- Updating 2-3 file paths in scripts\n- Adding `docs` to strip lists\n\n### Decision 2: Flatten the docs repo structure\n\n**Chosen**: Move `hankweave-docs/nextra-site/*` contents directly into `docs/` (not `docs/nextra-site/`).\n\n**Reasoning**: The `nextra-site/` wrapper exists because the docs repo has a root with README, notes, and plan directories. In the monorepo, these don't need a separate layer. The `package.json`, `tsconfig.json`, `next.config.mjs`, and all source files go directly in `docs/`.\n\n**Alternative considered**: Keeping `docs/nextra-site/` as-is. This preserves the original structure but adds an unnecessary level of nesting. It also means `\"workspaces\": [\"docs/nextra-site\"]` which is less clean.\n\n**What to do with `hankweave-docs/` non-nextra files**: The `README.md` at docs repo root, `notes/`, and `plan/` directories are development artifacts. They can either:\n- Be discarded (they're internal planning docs)\n- Be moved to an `intermediates/` entry documenting the docs site\n- Be kept at `docs/` level alongside the nextra files\n\n**Recommendation**: Discard `notes/` and `plan/` (they're historical planning docs). Keep the `README.md` as `docs/README.md` if desired, but it's not required.\n\n### Decision 3: Don't use `workspace:*` dependencies\n\n**Chosen**: No workspace dependency between docs and runtime.\n\n**Reasoning**: The docs site doesn't import from the runtime. The only data flow is schema JSON files copied by `generate-schemas.ts`. This is a script-level file copy, not a package dependency. Adding a `workspace:*` dependency would incorrectly suggest the docs depend on the runtime as a package.\n\n**When to revisit**: If the docs site ever needs to import TypeScript types or utilities from the runtime (e.g., for auto-generated API reference), add `workspace:*` at that point.\n\n### Decision 4: Convert the existing hankweave repo, don't create new\n\n**Chosen**: Modify `SouthBridgeAI/hankweave` in-place to become the monorepo.\n\n**Reasoning**: All CI workflows, GitHub secrets, branch protection rules, Linear integration, and team permissions are already configured on this repo. Creating a new repo would require reconfiguring everything. The existing git history stays intact. The docs files are brought in via a merge commit (or `git subtree add` if history preservation is desired).\n\n**Impact**: No disruption to existing CI/CD. No need to update secrets, protections, or integrations.\n\n### Decision 5: No Turborepo\n\n**Chosen**: Use Bun workspaces alone, no Turborepo.\n\n**Reasoning**: Two packages don't need task orchestration or caching. The runtime builds and tests independently. The docs site builds independently on Vercel. There's no dependency chain requiring `^build` semantics. Adding Turborepo would increase complexity with no clear benefit.\n\n**When to revisit**: If more packages are added (e.g., a shared types package, a CLI package), or if CI times become a problem and caching would help.\n\n### Decision 6: Single lockfile at root\n\n**Chosen**: One `bun.lock` at the repo root, replacing both existing lockfiles.\n\n**Reasoning**: Bun workspaces uses a single root lockfile. Both workspace packages' dependencies are resolved and locked together. This is how Bun workspaces work by default — there's no option for per-workspace lockfiles.\n\n**Impact**: The `bun.lock` from `hankweave` and the `bun.lock` from `hankweave-docs/nextra-site` are both replaced by a regenerated root `bun.lock`. The first `bun install` after migration will generate this.\n\n### Over/under engineering line\n\n- **Under**: Not adding path filtering to CI (docs changes trigger runtime tests). Acceptable for now — CI runs are fast and infrequent enough.\n- **Under**: Not using Bun catalogs for shared TypeScript version. Acceptable — the projects only share TypeScript and it's fine to have independent versions.\n- **Over**: Adding Turborepo, shared configs, workspace link scripts, or monorepo-specific CI tooling. Not needed for two packages.\n- **Right level**: Adding `docs` to strip lists, updating one path in `generate-schemas.ts`, updating the SOP doc. Minimal changes that make the monorepo work.\n\n---\n\n## f. The Actual Implementation Plan\n\n### Phase 0: Preparation\n\n**Step 0.1: Create a feature branch**\n```bash\ngit checkout develop\ngit pull origin develop\ngit checkout -b hrishi/monorepo-migration  # or similar\n```\n\n**Step 0.2: Verify clean state**\n- Ensure all tests pass: `bun run test:unit`, `bun run lint`, `bun run tc`\n- Ensure working directory is clean: `git status`\n\n### Phase 1: Bring docs into the repo\n\n**Step 1.1: Create the `docs/` directory**\n\nCopy the contents of `hankweave-docs/nextra-site/` into `docs/` at the runtime repo root.\n\nFiles to copy from `hankweave-docs/nextra-site/`:\n```\napp/\ncomponents/\ncontent/\npublic/\nstyles/\nbun.lock           (will be removed later — root lockfile takes over)\nmdx-components.tsx\nnext-env.d.ts\nnext.config.mjs\npackage.json\ntsconfig.json\nvercel.json\n```\n\nFiles from `hankweave-docs/` root to optionally include:\n```\n.gitignore          → merge relevant rules into root .gitignore\nREADME.md           → can include as docs/README.md (internal docs readme)\n```\n\nFiles to NOT copy:\n```\nnotes/              → historical planning docs, discard\nplan/               → historical planning docs, discard\n```\n\n**Step 1.2: Add workspace configuration to root `package.json`**\n\nModify `source/hankweave/package.json` — add `workspaces` field:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"name\": \"@southbridgeai/hankweave\",\n  \"version\": \"0.5.6\",\n  \"workspaces\": [\"docs\"],\n  ...\n}\n```\n\n**Step 1.3: Delete the docs-side `bun.lock`**\n\nRemove `docs/bun.lock` (the old `nextra-site/bun.lock`). The root lockfile will manage all dependencies.\n\n**Step 1.4: Run `bun install`**\n\nFrom the repo root, run `bun install`. This:\n- Resolves dependencies for both workspaces\n- Generates a new unified `bun.lock` at root\n- Creates a single `node_modules/` tree\n\nVerify: `docs/node_modules` should be a symlink or the workspace should be hoisted.\n\n**Step 1.5: Verify docs build locally**\n\n```bash\ncd docs && bun run build\n```\n\nVerify the Next.js build succeeds. If there are issues with module resolution, they'll surface here.\n\n### Phase 2: Update cross-repo references\n\n**Step 2.1: Update `scripts/generate-schemas.ts`**\n\nChange the `DOCS_SCHEMA_DIR` path from:\n```typescript\nconst DOCS_SCHEMA_DIR = path.join(\n  import.meta.dir,\n  \"..\",\n  \"..\",\n  \"hankweave-docs\",\n  \"nextra-site\",\n  \"public\",\n  \"schemas\",\n);\n```\n\nTo:\n```typescript\nconst DOCS_SCHEMA_DIR = path.join(\n  import.meta.dir,\n  \"..\",\n  \"docs\",\n  \"public\",\n  \"schemas\",\n);\n```\n\nThis is the only code change needed to formalize the cross-package dependency. The `docsAvailable` check at line ~77 will now reliably find the docs directory (it's always present in the monorepo, not dependent on sibling checkout).\n\n**Step 2.2: Run `bun run generate-schemas` to verify**\n\nVerify schemas are written to both `schemas/` and `docs/public/schemas/`.\n\n### Phase 3: Update strip lists for public sync\n\n**Step 3.1: Update `scripts/sync-public.ts` STRIP_DIRS**\n\nAt line 28, add `\"docs\"` to the STRIP_DIRS array:\n```typescript\nconst STRIP_DIRS = [\"intermediates\", \"external-docs\", \"public-release-files\", \".github/actions\", \"docs\"];\n```\n\nThis ensures the docs workspace doesn't end up in the public runtime snapshot.\n\n**Step 3.2: Update `scripts/release.ts` INTERNAL_FILES_TO_STRIP**\n\nAt line 54, add `\"docs/\"` to the strip list:\n```typescript\nconst INTERNAL_FILES_TO_STRIP = [\n  \"intermediates/\",\n  \"external-docs/\",\n  \"CLAUDE.md\",\n  \"CONTRIBUTING.md\",\n  \"standard-operating-procedures.md\",\n  \"docs/\",\n];\n```\n\nThis ensures the docs workspace is stripped from `release/alpha` during the release process.\n\n**Step 3.3: Update `scripts/test-sync-local.ts` verification list**\n\nAt line 160, add `\"docs\"` to the `shouldNotExist` array:\n```typescript\nconst shouldNotExist = [\n  \"intermediates\",\n  \"external-docs\",\n  // ... existing entries ...\n  \"docs\",  // Docs workspace should not be in public repo\n];\n```\n\n### Phase 4: Update .gitignore\n\n**Step 4.1: Merge docs-specific ignores into root `.gitignore`**\n\nAdd the following to the root `.gitignore` (from `hankweave-docs/.gitignore`):\n\n```gitignore\n# Docs build output\ndocs/.next/\ndocs/out/\n\n# Pagefind search index (generated at build time)\ndocs/public/_pagefind/\n```\n\nMost entries from the docs `.gitignore` (like `node_modules/`, `.env`, `.DS_Store`) are already covered by the root `.gitignore`.\n\n### Phase 5: Update documentation\n\n**Step 5.1: Update `standard-operating-procedures.md` repository structure**\n\nUpdate the repo structure section (lines 10-30) to reflect the monorepo:\n\n```\nhankweave-dev/              ← private repo (SouthBridgeAI/hankweave)\n├── server/                 ← runtime source code (TypeScript, Bun)\n├── scripts/                ← build, release, sync, and dev tooling\n├── tests/                  ← unit, integration, e2e, cross-runtime\n├── schemas/                ← generated JSON schemas (from Zod)\n├── shims/                  ← agent harness adapters\n├── docs/                   ← documentation site (Next.js + Nextra, Bun workspace)\n│   ├── app/                ← Next.js app directory\n│   ├── content/            ← MDX documentation pages\n│   ├── components/         ← custom React components\n│   ├── public/             ← static assets, schemas, images\n│   └── package.json        ← hankweave-docs workspace\n├── learning/               ← example hanks and guides (ships to public)\n├── intermediates/          ← planning docs, numbered by feature (internal only)\n├── external-docs/          ← SDK reference docs for agent context (internal only)\n├── public-release-files/   ← files overlaid onto the public repo during sync\n├── assets/                 ← images, demo GIFs\n├── .github/                ← CI workflows and composite actions\n├── CHANGELOG.md            ← Keep a Changelog format, [Unreleased] workflow\n├── CONTRIBUTING.md         ← branch model and PR guidelines (internal only)\n└── package.json            ← @southbridgeai/hankweave (private npm) + workspace root\n```\n\n**Step 5.2: Update the \"What gets stripped\" section**\n\nAdd `docs/` to the list of stripped directories.\n\n**Step 5.3: Update the schema generation note**\n\nIf there's any documentation about the cross-repo schema dependency, update it to note that schemas are now generated directly into `docs/public/schemas/` (same repo).\n\n### Phase 6: Verify everything works\n\n**Step 6.1: Run the full test suite**\n```bash\nbun run lint\nbun run tc\nbun run test:unit\nbun run test:integration\n```\n\n**Step 6.2: Run schema generation**\n```bash\nbun run generate-schemas\n```\nVerify output in both `schemas/` and `docs/public/schemas/`.\n\n**Step 6.3: Run the build**\n```bash\nbun run build\n```\nVerify `dist/index.js` is created.\n\n**Step 6.4: Verify docs build**\n```bash\ncd docs && bun run build\n```\n\n**Step 6.5: Test the release dry-run**\n```bash\nbun scripts/release.ts patch --dry-run --skip-checks\n```\nVerify validation passes (if on develop with clean tree).\n\n**Step 6.6: Test the sync dry-run**\n```bash\nbun scripts/test-sync-local.ts --version <current-version> --use-head\n```\nVerify `docs/` is correctly stripped from the snapshot and the verification checks pass.\n\n### Phase 7: Vercel configuration (out-of-band)\n\n**Step 7.1: Update Vercel project settings**\n\nThis is a manual change in the Vercel dashboard, not a code change:\n\n1. Go to the Vercel project for `hankweave-docs`\n2. Go to Settings → General → Root Directory\n3. Change from the current setting (either `/` or `nextra-site/`) to `docs/`\n4. Verify the Framework Preset is still \"Next.js\"\n5. The `vercel.json` inside `docs/` will continue to work\n\n**Step 7.2: Connect Vercel to the monorepo**\n\nIf the Vercel project is currently connected to `SouthBridgeAI/hankweave-docs`:\n1. Disconnect from `hankweave-docs`\n2. Connect to `SouthBridgeAI/hankweave`\n3. Set Root Directory to `docs/`\n4. Vercel will deploy when changes are pushed to the configured branch\n\nNote: Vercel can be configured to only deploy when files in the `docs/` directory change, using the \"Ignored Build Step\" setting: `git diff --quiet HEAD^ HEAD -- docs/`.\n\n### Phase 8: Commit and PR\n\n**Step 8.1: Commit the migration**\n\nCommit all changes as a single atomic commit on the feature branch:\n```\nMigrate to monorepo: bring docs into workspace\n\n- Add docs/ workspace (formerly SouthBridgeAI/hankweave-docs)\n- Add \"workspaces\" field to root package.json\n- Update generate-schemas.ts to use monorepo-local docs path\n- Add docs/ to strip lists (sync-public.ts, release.ts, test-sync-local.ts)\n- Update .gitignore for docs build artifacts\n- Update standard-operating-procedures.md\n```\n\n**Step 8.2: Open PR targeting develop**\n\nFollow existing PR conventions:\n- Title: `Monorepo migration: consolidate hankweave-docs into workspace`\n- Body: Problem/approach/files format per SOP section 4\n\n---\n\n## g. Testing Considerations\n\n### Existing tests affected\n\n**None of the existing unit, integration, or E2E tests should be affected.** The runtime code is unchanged — no server files, no type files, no config files are modified. Only scripts and metadata files are touched.\n\nThe tests that could theoretically be affected:\n\n1. **`tests/unit/json-schema.test.ts`** — Tests that schemas validate correctly. These read from `schemas/` directory which is unchanged.\n2. **E2E init tests** — Test the `--init` scaffolding flow. The runtime code is unchanged so these should pass.\n3. **Any test that reads `package.json`** — The only change is adding a `\"workspaces\"` field, which shouldn't affect anything that reads version, name, or dependencies.\n\n### New tests needed\n\nNo new automated tests are strictly required. The migration is a structural change, not a code change. Verification is manual:\n\n1. `bun install` succeeds at root and installs both workspaces\n2. `bun run build` still produces correct dist output\n3. `bun run generate-schemas` writes to both locations\n4. `docs/` build works: `cd docs && bun run build`\n5. Sync dry-run strips `docs/` correctly\n\n### Edge cases to consider\n\n1. **npm publish from root** — `npm publish` uses `\"files\"` field. The `docs/` directory is NOT in the `\"files\"` array (`[\"dist\", \"shims\", \"schemas\"]`), so it won't be included in the npm package. Verify this with `npm pack --dry-run`.\n2. **CI `bun install --frozen-lockfile`** — The new unified `bun.lock` needs to be committed. If it's not, CI will fail because `--frozen-lockfile` doesn't allow lockfile generation.\n3. **`.npmignore` and docs** — The `.npmignore` doesn't need `docs/` added because the `\"files\"` field in `package.json` is an allowlist — only listed paths are included.\n\n---\n\n## h. Documentation Considerations\n\n### Docs to update\n\n1. **`standard-operating-procedures.md`** — Repository structure diagram, strip list documentation (see Phase 5)\n2. **This plan itself** — Should be filed as an intermediate (e.g., `intermediates/45-monorepo-migration/`)\n\n### New docs needed\n\nNone required. The monorepo migration is an internal infrastructure change. The docs site content (`docs/content/`) doesn't need updating — it documents Hankweave the product, not the development repo structure.\n\n### Docs that should NOT be updated\n\n- The user-facing docs (`docs/content/guides/getting-started.mdx`, etc.) — these reference `hankweave` as a CLI tool, not as a repo structure\n- The public `README.md` (in `public-release-files/`) — this describes the public `hankweave-runtime` repo, not the private dev repo\n\n---\n\n## i. Additional Resources\n\n### Bun workspaces\n\n- [bun.sh/docs/install/workspaces](https://bun.sh/docs/install/workspaces) — primary reference\n- Root package can be a workspace member itself (important for our \"runtime at root\" approach)\n- `bun install` at root installs all workspace dependencies\n- `bun --filter <name> <script>` runs scripts in specific workspaces (useful for `bun --filter hankweave-docs build`)\n\n### Vercel monorepo support\n\n- Vercel's Root Directory setting specifies which subdirectory contains the deployable project\n- \"Ignored Build Step\" can filter deploys to only docs changes\n\n### Git subtree (optional history preservation)\n\nIf docs history is desired:\n```bash\ngit remote add -f hankweave-docs <url>\ngit merge -s ours --no-commit --allow-unrelated-histories hankweave-docs/main\ngit read-tree --prefix=docs/ -u hankweave-docs/main\ngit commit -m \"Merge hankweave-docs into monorepo as docs workspace\"\n```\n\nThis preserves the full commit history of `hankweave-docs` at the `docs/` prefix. After this, remove the files from `hankweave-docs/` root that aren't needed (README, notes/, plan/) and restructure so the nextra-site contents are at `docs/` root.\n\nAlternatively, a simple file copy with a \"bring docs into monorepo\" commit is simpler and the docs repo's history is available in the original repo if ever needed.\n\n---\n\n## Summary of Changes\n\n| File | Change Type | Description |\n|------|-------------|-------------|\n| `package.json` | Modify | Add `\"workspaces\": [\"docs\"]` |\n| `docs/` | Create (directory) | Contains contents of `hankweave-docs/nextra-site/` |\n| `scripts/generate-schemas.ts` | Modify | Update `DOCS_SCHEMA_DIR` path (line 24-32) |\n| `scripts/sync-public.ts` | Modify | Add `\"docs\"` to `STRIP_DIRS` (line 28) |\n| `scripts/release.ts` | Modify | Add `\"docs/\"` to `INTERNAL_FILES_TO_STRIP` (line 54) |\n| `scripts/test-sync-local.ts` | Modify | Add `\"docs\"` to `shouldNotExist` (line 160) |\n| `.gitignore` | Modify | Add docs build artifacts |\n| `standard-operating-procedures.md` | Modify | Update repo structure diagram |\n| `bun.lock` | Regenerate | New unified lockfile |\n\n**Total files modified**: 7 existing files + 1 new directory of ~30 files\n**Total lines of code changed**: ~20 lines across scripts + SOP update\n\nThis is deliberately minimal. The migration's success is measured by how little changes while still achieving the goal: a reliable, explicit relationship between runtime and docs in a single repo.\n","structuredPatch":[],"originalFile":null},"timestamp":"2026-03-05T12:17:56.399Z"}
