{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01DMo1S6fGuTQ2QMve3S2XqG","type":"tool_result","content":"     1→     1→# Task: Migrate Hankweave to Monorepo\n     2→     2→\n     3→     3→## Intent\n     4→     4→\n     5→     5→From the task specification in `read_only_data_source/task.md`:\n     6→     6→\n     7→     7→> \"Migrate the Hankweave project from its current multi-repository structure into a unified monorepo. This includes the runtime (`hankweave`), documentation (`hankweave-docs`), and any related packages/tools.\"\n     8→     8→\n     9→     9→The stated goals are:\n    10→    10→\n    11→    11→> - Consolidate all Hankweave repositories into a single monorepo\n    12→    12→> - Maintain clean package boundaries with proper workspace configuration\n    13→    13→> - Preserve git history where practical\n    14→    14→> - Ensure CI/CD pipelines work with the new structure\n    15→    15→> - Keep the developer experience smooth (build, test, lint across packages)\n    16→    16→\n    17→    17→Stated considerations from the task spec:\n    18→    18→\n    19→    19→> - Bun workspaces vs other monorepo tooling (turborepo, nx, etc.)\n    20→    20→> - Package publishing strategy (npm packages from monorepo)\n    21→    21→> - Schema sharing between packages\n    22→    22→> - Test infrastructure consolidation\n    23→    23→> - Documentation site build integration\n    24→    24→> - Binary build pipeline for cross-platform releases\n    25→    25→\n    26→    26→---\n    27→    27→\n    28→    28→## Context\n    29→    29→\n    30→    30→### What exists today\n    31→    31→\n    32→    32→There are currently three repos involved in the Hankweave release pipeline:\n    33→    33→\n    34→    34→1. **`SouthBridgeAI/hankweave`** (private) — the core TypeScript/Bun runtime. Package `@southbridgeai/hankweave`, currently at `v0.5.6`. All active development happens here on a `develop → release/alpha` branch model.\n    35→    35→\n    36→    36→2. **`SouthBridgeAI/hankweave-docs`** (separate private repo) — the documentation site. Next.js + Nextra 4.x, deployed to Vercel. Package name `hankweave-docs`, version `2.0.0`.\n    37→    37→\n    38→    38→3. **`SouthBridgeAI/hankweave-runtime`** (public) — receives sanitized snapshots from the private runtime repo via a `sync-public` GitHub Actions workflow. Publishes the public `hankweave` npm package accessible as `bunx hankweave` / `npx hankweave`.\n    39→    39→\n    40→    40→### The already-existing cross-repo dependency (key finding)\n    41→    41→\n    42→    42→There is a live implicit dependency between the two dev repos that makes this migration particularly motivated: `hankweave/scripts/generate-schemas.ts` (lines 26–31) already reaches into the docs repo:\n    43→    43→\n    44→    44→```typescript\n    45→    45→const DOCS_SCHEMA_DIR = path.join(\n    46→    46→  import.meta.dir, \"..\", \"..\",\n    47→    47→  \"hankweave-docs\", \"nextra-site\", \"public\", \"schemas\",\n    48→    48→);\n    49→    49→```\n    50→    50→\n    51→    51→This path hardcodes the assumption that both repos are checked out as siblings. The script silently skips writing to docs if the path doesn't exist. A monorepo would make this relationship explicit and reliable rather than fragile and implicit.\n    52→    52→\n    53→    53→### Why this came up\n    54→    54→\n    55→    55→The task description states: \"Coordinating changes across repos creates friction - a feature that touches both runtime and docs requires multiple PRs, version bumps, and careful sequencing. A monorepo would simplify cross-cutting changes and dependency management.\"\n    56→    56→\n    57→    57→The schema generation dependency is a concrete example of that friction — it only works if both repos happen to be checked out side-by-side on a developer's machine.\n    58→    58→\n    59→    59→### Is there related existing work?\n    60→    60→\n    61→    61→No monorepo-related intermediates were found in `source/hankweave/intermediates/`. The existing planning docs (numbered up to 44) cover other feature areas. This appears to be a net-new task.\n    62→    62→\n    63→    63→No Linear ticket ID was present in the task data source. The project uses `ENG-` prefixes for Linear tickets (visible in the CHANGELOG and PR titles). This task may have a ticket, but it wasn't included in the provided data.\n    64→    64→\n    65→    65→---\n    66→    66→\n    67→    67→## Why\n    68→    68→\n    69→    69→The primary driver is developer-experience friction from coordinating changes across two repos. The schema generation already implicitly assumes the repos are co-located — a monorepo formalizes what's already partially true.\n    70→    70→\n    71→    71→Secondary motivations: shared tooling (Biome config, TypeScript, Bun), simplified CI path filtering, and a single place to understand the full project structure. With a monorepo, a PR that adds a new runtime feature and updates its documentation can be a single atomic change.\n    72→    72→\n    73→    73→---\n    74→    74→\n    75→    75→## Questions\n    76→    76→\n    77→    77→These are open questions that need answering before or during implementation. They represent the real complexity of this task.\n    78→    78→\n    79→    79→### Q1: What happens to the public release pipeline?\n    80→    80→\n    81→    81→The most significant open question. The current pipeline is:\n    82→    82→\n    83→    83→```\n    84→    84→hankweave (private dev repo) → sync-public workflow → hankweave-runtime (public mirror)\n    85→    85→```\n    86→    86→\n    87→    87→The docs repo (`hankweave-docs`) does **not** go through this pipeline. It deploys directly to Vercel.\n    88→    88→\n    89→    89→In a monorepo:\n    90→    90→- Does the monorepo have a single private → public sync, or separate ones per package?\n    91→    91→- Does the docs package still deploy directly from the monorepo to Vercel (Vercel supports this with root directory configuration)?\n    92→    92→- Does `SouthBridgeAI/hankweave-runtime` still exist as a separate public mirror, or does the monorepo eventually become the public-facing source?\n    93→    93→- Does `SouthBridgeAI/hankweave-docs` cease to exist as an independent repo?\n    94→    94→\n    95→    95→The `scripts/sync-public.ts` strips many internal files before syncing. In a monorepo, the sync probably continues to only push the runtime package tree — but the strip list may need to account for the new directory structure.\n    96→    96→\n    97→    97→### Q2: What is the monorepo root structure?\n    98→    98→\n    99→    99→Options include:\n   100→   100→- Flat: `packages/runtime/` and `packages/docs/`\n   101→   101→- Named: `packages/hankweave/` and `packages/hankweave-docs/`\n   102→   102→- Apps-style: `packages/hankweave/` and `apps/docs/` (distinguishing publishable from deployable)\n   103→   103→\n   104→   104→The `apps/` vs `packages/` convention (common in Turborepo docs) distinguishes npm-publishable packages from deployable apps. Since only the runtime publishes to npm, this split would be natural. But it also adds a directory depth that may not be worth it for two packages.\n   105→   105→\n   106→   106→The question of whether the runtime stays at the repo root or moves into a subdirectory is also open. Keeping the runtime at root with docs in `docs/` is the minimal-disruption approach.\n   107→   107→\n   108→   108→### Q3: Should a new repo be created, or should an existing one be converted?\n   109→   109→\n   110→   110→Options:\n   111→   111→- **Create a new repo** (e.g., `SouthBridgeAI/hankweave-dev`) and bring both repos in\n   112→   112→- **Convert `SouthBridgeAI/hankweave`** to be the monorepo root (add docs as a workspace, minimal disruption to existing CI/release infrastructure)\n   113→   113→- **Use git subtree** to merge docs history into the runtime repo as a subdirectory\n   114→   114→\n   115→   115→Converting the runtime repo to be the monorepo root is the lowest-friction path: all CI workflows, secrets, branch protection rules, and Linear integrations stay in place.\n   116→   116→\n   117→   117→### Q4: What changes in the internal strip list?\n   118→   118→\n   119→   119→`scripts/release.ts` and `scripts/sync-public.ts` both maintain lists of files/directories to strip before the public sync:\n   120→   120→- `intermediates/`, `external-docs/`, `public-release-files/`, `CLAUDE.md`, `CONTRIBUTING.md`, etc.\n   121→   121→\n   122→   122→In a monorepo, the docs workspace (wherever it lands) is also internal-only and should not appear in the public runtime snapshot. The strip list needs updating.\n   123→   123→\n   124→   124→### Q5: What changes in CI?\n   125→   125→\n   126→   126→Currently:\n   127→   127→- `ci.yml` runs on `develop` and `release/alpha` pushes: lint/typecheck, unit/integration tests, E2E init tests\n   128→   128→- `release.yml` triggers on `v*` tags: npm publish + binary builds\n   129→   129→- `sync-public.yml` manually triggered for public sync\n   130→   130→- Vercel handles docs deployment separately\n   131→   131→\n   132→   132→In a monorepo, CI should ideally only run relevant jobs for changed packages. GitHub Actions supports path filtering with `paths:` triggers. The question is how granular to make this — for two packages, running everything on every push may still be acceptable.\n   133→   133→\n   134→   134→### Q6: How does Vercel handle the docs site in a monorepo?\n   135→   135→\n   136→   136→Vercel supports monorepos by letting you specify the \"Root Directory\" in project settings. The current `vercel.json` in `nextra-site/` would stay in place relative to the Next.js project root. The Vercel project would need to be reconfigured to point to the new path (e.g., `apps/docs/nextra-site/` or `docs/nextra-site/`).\n   137→   137→\n   138→   138→### Q7: Versioning strategy?\n   139→   139→\n   140→   140→The runtime is versioned independently (`0.5.6`). The docs site is `2.0.0` and doesn't publish to npm. Independent versioning per package is almost certainly correct — the runtime and docs don't release in lockstep, and semver on the runtime is meaningful for users while the docs version is internal.\n   141→   141→\n   142→   142→### Q8: Should `workspace:*` link docs to runtime?\n   143→   143→\n   144→   144→If the docs site ever needs to import from the runtime (e.g., for auto-generated API reference or schema types), a `workspace:*` dependency would make this a proper workspace dependency. Currently the only cross-package dependency is a path-based write in `generate-schemas.ts`. Whether to formalize this as a workspace dependency or keep it as a script-level path reference is a decision point.\n   145→   145→\n   146→   146→### Q9: What is the scope of \"related packages/tools\"?\n   147→   147→\n   148→   148→The task mentions \"any related packages/tools.\" The `tools/linear-pull` in this agent workspace is a planning utility, not part of the hankweave source. Within the hankweave repo, `scripts/`, `shims/`, and `schemas/` are part of the runtime package, not separate packages. It's worth confirming with the task author whether there are any other tools or packages not visible in the current source snapshot.\n   149→   149→\n   150→   150→---\n   151→   151→\n   152→   152→## Research\n   153→   153→\n   154→   154→### Bun Workspaces\n   155→   155→\n   156→   156→Bun natively supports workspaces via the standard `workspaces` field in `package.json`. For a two-package monorepo:\n   157→   157→\n   158→   158→```json\n   159→   159→{\n   160→   160→  \"name\": \"hankweave-dev\",\n   161→   161→  \"workspaces\": [\"packages/*\"]\n   162→   162→}\n   163→   163→```\n   164→   164→\n   165→   165→Cross-package references use the `workspace:` protocol (`\"workspace:*\"`). Bun's **Catalogs** feature shares dependency versions across packages via `catalog:` protocol — useful for ensuring runtime and docs agree on shared tools like TypeScript.\n   166→   166→\n   167→   167→Full docs: [bun.sh/docs/install/workspaces](https://bun.sh/docs/install/workspaces), [bun.sh/docs/install/catalogs](https://bun.sh/docs/install/catalogs)\n   168→   168→\n   169→   169→### Turborepo\n   170→   170→\n   171→   171→Turborepo adds task orchestration and caching on top of workspaces. A `turbo.json` defines task pipelines with dependency ordering (`^build` = build dependencies first). For a two-package monorepo, this is likely overkill initially — but can be adopted incrementally. Worth noting: Turborepo supports incremental adoption, meaning you can start with one task and expand.\n   172→   172→\n   173→   173→Full docs: [turborepo.dev/repo/docs/getting-started/add-to-existing-repository](https://turborepo.dev/repo/docs/getting-started/add-to-existing-repository)\n   174→   174→\n   175→   175→### Git History Preservation\n   176→   176→\n   177→   177→`git subtree add` can merge another repo into a subdirectory while preserving its full commit history:\n   178→   178→\n   179→   179→```bash\n   180→   180→git remote add -f hankweave-docs <url>\n   181→   181→git merge -s ours --no-commit --allow-unrelated-histories hankweave-docs/main\n   182→   182→git read-tree --prefix=apps/docs/ -u hankweave-docs/main\n   183→   183→git commit -m \"Merge hankweave-docs into monorepo\"\n   184→   184→```\n   185→   185→\n   186→   186→Given that `hankweave-runtime` (public mirror) already receives snapshots without full history, and the docs repo has no complex history of its own, preserving history is \"nice to have\" but not critical.\n   187→   187→\n   188→   188→Full docs: [docs.github.com/en/get-started/using-git/about-git-subtree-merges](https://docs.github.com/en/get-started/using-git/about-git-subtree-merges)\n   189→   189→\n   190→   190→---\n   191→   191→\n   192→   192→## References\n   193→   193→\n   194→   194→### Code references\n   195→   195→\n   196→   196→| File | Path | Notes |\n   197→   197→|------|------|-------|\n   198→   198→| Runtime `package.json` | `source/hankweave/package.json` | `@southbridgeai/hankweave`, v0.5.6 |\n   199→   199→| Docs `package.json` | `source/hankweave-docs/nextra-site/package.json` | `hankweave-docs`, v2.0.0 |\n   200→   200→| Cross-repo schema path | `source/hankweave/scripts/generate-schemas.ts:26-31` | Key existing cross-repo dependency |\n   201→   201→| Release script | `source/hankweave/scripts/release.ts` | Branch model, strip list |\n   202→   202→| Sync-to-public script | `source/hankweave/scripts/sync-public.ts` | Strip list, package transform |\n   203→   203→| Standard operating procedures | `source/hankweave/standard-operating-procedures.md` | Branch model, AI agent policy, repo structure |\n   204→   204→| CI workflow | `source/hankweave/.github/workflows/ci.yml` | Test matrix, composite actions |\n   205→   205→| Release workflow | `source/hankweave/.github/workflows/release.yml` | npm + binaries + GH release |\n   206→   206→| Sync-public workflow | `source/hankweave/.github/workflows/sync-public.yml` | Private → public sync |\n   207→   207→| Biome config | `source/hankweave/biome.json` | Covers `server/`, `tests/`, `types/` |\n   208→   208→| Vercel config | `source/hankweave-docs/nextra-site/vercel.json` | Root directory config |\n   209→   209→| Bun config | `source/hankweave/bunfig.toml` | Test env vars |\n   210→   210→\n   211→   211→### Background\n   212→   212→\n   213→   213→Full research and context compiled in `plan/background/background.md`.\n   214→   214→\n   215→\n   216→<system-reminder>\n   217→Whenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n   218→</system-reminder>\n   219→\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"6f6abf3a-0b5f-4251-a578-7f2d66ac9b9a","uuid":"a5e4cf8e-8ccb-467b-a084-504bd7ce6c73","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/philip/.claude/projects/-Users-philip-projects-hw-budget-tests-plan-gen-execution-agentRoot/6f6abf3a-0b5f-4251-a578-7f2d66ac9b9a/tool-results/toolu_018s9BErLdexcVYmLC1g5bYG.txt","content":"     1→# Task: Migrate Hankweave to Monorepo\n     2→\n     3→## Intent\n     4→\n     5→From the task specification in `read_only_data_source/task.md`:\n     6→\n     7→> \"Migrate the Hankweave project from its current multi-repository structure into a unified monorepo. This includes the runtime (`hankweave`), documentation (`hankweave-docs`), and any related packages/tools.\"\n     8→\n     9→The stated goals are:\n    10→\n    11→> - Consolidate all Hankweave repositories into a single monorepo\n    12→> - Maintain clean package boundaries with proper workspace configuration\n    13→> - Preserve git history where practical\n    14→> - Ensure CI/CD pipelines work with the new structure\n    15→> - Keep the developer experience smooth (build, test, lint across packages)\n    16→\n    17→Stated considerations from the task spec:\n    18→\n    19→> - Bun workspaces vs other monorepo tooling (turborepo, nx, etc.)\n    20→> - Package publishing strategy (npm packages from monorepo)\n    21→> - Schema sharing between packages\n    22→> - Test infrastructure consolidation\n    23→> - Documentation site build integration\n    24→> - Binary build pipeline for cross-platform releases\n    25→\n    26→---\n    27→\n    28→## Context\n    29→\n    30→### What exists today\n    31→\n    32→There are currently three repos involved in the Hankweave release pipeline:\n    33→\n    34→1. **`SouthBridgeAI/hankweave`** (private) — the core TypeScript/Bun runtime. Package `@southbridgeai/hankweave`, currently at `v0.5.6`. All active development happens here on a `develop → release/alpha` branch model.\n    35→\n    36→2. **`SouthBridgeAI/hankweave-docs`** (separate private repo) — the documentation site. Next.js + Nextra 4.x, deployed to Vercel. Package name `hankweave-docs`, version `2.0.0`.\n    37→\n    38→3. **`SouthBridgeAI/hankweave-runtime`** (public) — receives sanitized snapshots from the private runtime repo via a `sync-public` GitHub Actions workflow. Publishes the public `hankweave` npm package accessible as `bunx hankweave` / `npx hankweave`.\n    39→\n    40→### The already-existing cross-repo dependency (key finding)\n    41→\n    42→There is a live implicit dependency between the two dev repos that makes this migration particularly motivated: `hankweave/scripts/generate-schemas.ts` (lines 26–31) already reaches into the docs repo:\n    43→\n    44→```typescript\n    45→const DOCS_SCHEMA_DIR = path.join(\n    46→  import.meta.dir, \"..\", \"..\",\n    47→  \"hankweave-docs\", \"nextra-site\", \"public\", \"schemas\",\n    48→);\n    49→```\n    50→\n    51→This path hardcodes the assumption that both repos are checked out as siblings. The script silently skips writing to docs if the path doesn't exist. A monorepo would make this relationship explicit and reliable rather than fragile and implicit.\n    52→\n    53→### Why this came up\n    54→\n    55→The task description states: \"Coordinating changes across repos creates friction - a feature that touches both runtime and docs requires multiple PRs, version bumps, and careful sequencing. A monorepo would simplify cross-cutting changes and dependency management.\"\n    56→\n    57→The schema generation dependency is a concrete example of that friction — it only works if both repos happen to be checked out side-by-side on a developer's machine.\n    58→\n    59→### Is there related existing work?\n    60→\n    61→No monorepo-related intermediates were found in `source/hankweave/intermediates/`. The existing planning docs (numbered up to 44) cover other feature areas. This appears to be a net-new task.\n    62→\n    63→No Linear ticket ID was present in the task data source. The project uses `ENG-` prefixes for Linear tickets (visible in the CHANGELOG and PR titles). This task may have a ticket, but it wasn't included in the provided data.\n    64→\n    65→---\n    66→\n    67→## Why\n    68→\n    69→The primary driver is developer-experience friction from coordinating changes across two repos. The schema generation already implicitly assumes the repos are co-located — a monorepo formalizes what's already partially true.\n    70→\n    71→Secondary motivations: shared tooling (Biome config, TypeScript, Bun), simplified CI path filtering, and a single place to understand the full project structure. With a monorepo, a PR that adds a new runtime feature and updates its documentation can be a single atomic change.\n    72→\n    73→---\n    74→\n    75→## Questions\n    76→\n    77→These are open questions that need answering before or during implementation. They represent the real complexity of this task.\n    78→\n    79→### Q1: What happens to the public release pipeline?\n    80→\n    81→The most significant open question. The current pipeline is:\n    82→\n    83→```\n    84→hankweave (private dev repo) → sync-public workflow → hankweave-runtime (public mirror)\n    85→```\n    86→\n    87→The docs repo (`hankweave-docs`) does **not** go through this pipeline. It deploys directly to Vercel.\n    88→\n    89→In a monorepo:\n    90→- Does the monorepo have a single private → public sync, or separate ones per package?\n    91→- Does the docs package still deploy directly from the monorepo to Vercel (Vercel supports this with root directory configuration)?\n    92→- Does `SouthBridgeAI/hankweave-runtime` still exist as a separate public mirror, or does the monorepo eventually become the public-facing source?\n    93→- Does `SouthBridgeAI/hankweave-docs` cease to exist as an independent repo?\n    94→\n    95→The `scripts/sync-public.ts` strips many internal files before syncing. In a monorepo, the sync probably continues to only push the runtime package tree — but the strip list may need to account for the new directory structure.\n    96→\n    97→### Q2: What is the monorepo root structure?\n    98→\n    99→Options include:\n   100→- Flat: `packages/runtime/` and `packages/docs/`\n   101→- Named: `packages/hankweave/` and `packages/hankweave-docs/`\n   102→- Apps-style: `packages/hankweave/` and `apps/docs/` (distinguishing publishable from deployable)\n   103→\n   104→The `apps/` vs `packages/` convention (common in Turborepo docs) distinguishes npm-publishable packages from deployable apps. Since only the runtime publishes to npm, this split would be natural. But it also adds a directory depth that may not be worth it for two packages.\n   105→\n   106→The question of whether the runtime stays at the repo root or moves into a subdirectory is also open. Keeping the runtime at root with docs in `docs/` is the minimal-disruption approach.\n   107→\n   108→### Q3: Should a new repo be created, or should an existing one be converted?\n   109→\n   110→Options:\n   111→- **Create a new repo** (e.g., `SouthBridgeAI/hankweave-dev`) and bring both repos in\n   112→- **Convert `SouthBridgeAI/hankweave`** to be the monorepo root (add docs as a workspace, minimal disruption to existing CI/release infrastructure)\n   113→- **Use git subtree** to merge docs history into the runtime repo as a subdirectory\n   114→\n   115→Converting the runtime repo to be the monorepo root is the lowest-friction path: all CI workflows, secrets, branch protection rules, and Linear integrations stay in place.\n   116→\n   117→### Q4: What changes in the internal strip list?\n   118→\n   119→`scripts/release.ts` and `scripts/sync-public.ts` both maintain lists of files/directories to strip before the public sync:\n   120→- `intermediates/`, `external-docs/`, `public-release-files/`, `CLAUDE.md`, `CONTRIBUTING.md`, etc.\n   121→\n   122→In a monorepo, the docs workspace (wherever it lands) is also internal-only and should not appear in the public runtime snapshot. The strip list needs updating.\n   123→\n   124→### Q5: What changes in CI?\n   125→\n   126→Currently:\n   127→- `ci.yml` runs on `develop` and `release/alpha` pushes: lint/typecheck, unit/integration tests, E2E init tests\n   128→- `release.yml` triggers on `v*` tags: npm publish + binary builds\n   129→- `sync-public.yml` manually triggered for public sync\n   130→- Vercel handles docs deployment separately\n   131→\n   132→In a monorepo, CI should ideally only run relevant jobs for changed packages. GitHub Actions supports path filtering with `paths:` triggers. The question is how granular to make this — for two packages, running everything on every push may still be acceptable.\n   133→\n   134→### Q6: How does Vercel handle the docs site in a monorepo?\n   135→\n   136→Vercel supports monorepos by letting you specify the \"Root Directory\" in project settings. The current `vercel.json` in `nextra-site/` would stay in place relative to the Next.js project root. The Vercel project would need to be reconfigured to point to the new path (e.g., `apps/docs/nextra-site/` or `docs/nextra-site/`).\n   137→\n   138→### Q7: Versioning strategy?\n   139→\n   140→The runtime is versioned independently (`0.5.6`). The docs site is `2.0.0` and doesn't publish to npm. Independent versioning per package is almost certainly correct — the runtime and docs don't release in lockstep, and semver on the runtime is meaningful for users while the docs version is internal.\n   141→\n   142→### Q8: Should `workspace:*` link docs to runtime?\n   143→\n   144→If the docs site ever needs to import from the runtime (e.g., for auto-generated API reference or schema types), a `workspace:*` dependency would make this a proper workspace dependency. Currently the only cross-package dependency is a path-based write in `generate-schemas.ts`. Whether to formalize this as a workspace dependency or keep it as a script-level path reference is a decision point.\n   145→\n   146→### Q9: What is the scope of \"related packages/tools\"?\n   147→\n   148→The task mentions \"any related packages/tools.\" The `tools/linear-pull` in this agent workspace is a planning utility, not part of the hankweave source. Within the hankweave repo, `scripts/`, `shims/`, and `schemas/` are part of the runtime package, not separate packages. It's worth confirming with the task author whether there are any other tools or packages not visible in the current source snapshot.\n   149→\n   150→---\n   151→\n   152→## Research\n   153→\n   154→### Bun Workspaces\n   155→\n   156→Bun natively supports workspaces via the standard `workspaces` field in `package.json`. For a two-package monorepo:\n   157→\n   158→```json\n   159→{\n   160→  \"name\": \"hankweave-dev\",\n   161→  \"workspaces\": [\"packages/*\"]\n   162→}\n   163→```\n   164→\n   165→Cross-package references use the `workspace:` protocol (`\"workspace:*\"`). Bun's **Catalogs** feature shares dependency versions across packages via `catalog:` protocol — useful for ensuring runtime and docs agree on shared tools like TypeScript.\n   166→\n   167→Full docs: [bun.sh/docs/install/workspaces](https://bun.sh/docs/install/workspaces), [bun.sh/docs/install/catalogs](https://bun.sh/docs/install/catalogs)\n   168→\n   169→### Turborepo\n   170→\n   171→Turborepo adds task orchestration and caching on top of workspaces. A `turbo.json` defines task pipelines with dependency ordering (`^build` = build dependencies first). For a two-package monorepo, this is likely overkill initially — but can be adopted incrementally. Worth noting: Turborepo supports incremental adoption, meaning you can start with one task and expand.\n   172→\n   173→Full docs: [turborepo.dev/repo/docs/getting-started/add-to-existing-repository](https://turborepo.dev/repo/docs/getting-started/add-to-existing-repository)\n   174→\n   175→### Git History Preservation\n   176→\n   177→`git subtree add` can merge another repo into a subdirectory while preserving its full commit history:\n   178→\n   179→```bash\n   180→git remote add -f hankweave-docs <url>\n   181→git merge -s ours --no-commit --allow-unrelated-histories hankweave-docs/main\n   182→git read-tree --prefix=apps/docs/ -u hankweave-docs/main\n   183→git commit -m \"Merge hankweave-docs into monorepo\"\n   184→```\n   185→\n   186→Given that `hankweave-runtime` (public mirror) already receives snapshots without full history, and the docs repo has no complex history of its own, preserving history is \"nice to have\" but not critical.\n   187→\n   188→Full docs: [docs.github.com/en/get-started/using-git/about-git-subtree-merges](https://docs.github.com/en/get-started/using-git/about-git-subtree-merges)\n   189→\n   190→---\n   191→\n   192→## References\n   193→\n   194→### Code references\n   195→\n   196→| File | Path | Notes |\n   197→|------|------|-------|\n   198→| Runtime `package.json` | `source/hankweave/package.json` | `@southbridgeai/hankweave`, v0.5.6 |\n   199→| Docs `package.json` | `source/hankweave-docs/nextra-site/package.json` | `hankweave-docs`, v2.0.0 |\n   200→| Cross-repo schema path | `source/hankweave/scripts/generate-schemas.ts:26-31` | Key existing cross-repo dependency |\n   201→| Release script | `source/hankweave/scripts/release.ts` | Branch model, strip list |\n   202→| Sync-to-public script | `source/hankweave/scripts/sync-public.ts` | Strip list, package transform |\n   203→| Standard operating procedures | `source/hankweave/standard-operating-procedures.md` | Branch model, AI agent policy, repo structure |\n   204→| CI workflow | `source/hankweave/.github/workflows/ci.yml` | Test matrix, composite actions |\n   205→| Release workflow | `source/hankweave/.github/workflows/release.yml` | npm + binaries + GH release |\n   206→| Sync-public workflow | `source/hankweave/.github/workflows/sync-public.yml` | Private → public sync |\n   207→| Biome config | `source/hankweave/biome.json` | Covers `server/`, `tests/`, `types/` |\n   208→| Vercel config | `source/hankweave-docs/nextra-site/vercel.json` | Root directory config |\n   209→| Bun config | `source/hankweave/bunfig.toml` | Test env vars |\n   210→\n   211→### Background\n   212→\n   213→Full research and context compiled in `plan/background/background.md`.\n   214→\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n","numLines":219,"startLine":1,"totalLines":219}},"timestamp":"2026-03-05T12:18:03.650Z"}
